82 (72.3%)\n-4.7% (-0.9, \n10.4)\n\n57\n\nReference ID: 3989587\n\n\f\nClinical Review\nShrimant Mishra, MD, MPH (Efficacy)\nHiwot, Hiruy, MD, PhD (Safety)\nBLA 761046\nBezlotoxumab\n\nLooking at clinical cure in study P002, the MK-6072 arm performed better than the placebo arm, \nwith a difference in the rate of clinical cure between Placebo and MK-6072 of - 4.7%.  The \nplacebo cure rate was lower than that seen in the P001 study and the pivotal fidaxomicin trials.\n\nThe only subgroups (with sample size of at least 50) where the rate of clinical cure was higher in \nthe placebo arm was in Asian subjects, subjects enrolled in sites in the Asia/Pacific region, \nsubjects with a history of CDI in the prior 3 months, subjects with a history of CDI in the prior 6 \nmonths, and subjects with a baseline Zar score ≥2.  Using the same criteria as earlier to find \nsubgroups that performed more or less favorably compared with the difference seen in the FAS \npopulations (-4.7%%), MK-6072 performed “less’ favorably in these same subgroups. MK-6072 \nperformed more favorably in those with a compromised immunity at baseline and those who had \nreceived > 6 days of SOC at the time of study drug infusion.\n\nTable 21: Study P002 Subgroup Analysis of Clinical Cure Using Subgroup as Denominator \n(FAS)\n\nTreatment Group\n\n(N= )\nSubgroup Analysis\n\nMK-6072\n\nMK-3415A\n\nPlacebo \nGroup\n(N=378 )\n\n(N=395 )\n\n(N=390 )\n\nn (%)\n\nn (%)\n\nn (%)\nSex\n\nMale\n114/152 \n(75%)\n144/182 \n(79.1%)\n136/178 (76.4%)\n\nFemale\n180/226 \n(79.6%)\n\n182/213 \n(85.4%)\n146/212 (68.9%)\n\nAge Group\n\n < 65 years\n133/172 \n(77.3%)\n\n157/190 \n(82.6%)\n102/149 (68.5%)\n\n≥ 65 years\n161/206 \n(78.2%)\n\n169/205 \n(82.4%)\n\n180/241\n(74.7%)\n< 75 years\n206/262 \n(78.6%)\n\n234/281 \n(83.3%)\n166/241 (68.9%)\n\n≥ 75 years\n88/116 \n(75.9%)\n92/114 (80.7%)\n116/149 (77.8%)\n\nRace\n\nWhite\n240/309 \n(77.7%)\n\n259/311 \n(83.3%)\n215/305 (70.5%)\n\n58\n\nReference ID: 3989587\n\n\f\nClinical Review\nShrimant Mishra, MD, MPH (Efficacy)\nHiwot, Hiruy, MD, PhD (Safety)\nBLA 761046\nBezlotoxumab\n\nBlack or African \nAmerican\n6/10 (60%)\n14/17 (82.4%)\n12/18 (66.7%)\n\nAsian\n47/57 \n(82.5%)\n51/63 (81%)\n54/65 (83.1%)\n\nAmerican Indian or \nAlaska Native\n0/1 (0%)\n1/2 (50%)\n0/0 (0%)\n\nMultiple\n1/1 (100%)\n1/2 (50%)\n1/2 (50%)\nEthnicity\n\nHispanic or Latino\n30/45 \n(66.7%)\n25/33 (75.8%)\n23/33 (69.7%)\n\nRegion \n\nUS\n99/131 \n(75.6%)\n\n110/136 \n(80.9%)\n86/133 (64.7%)\n\nEx-US\n195/247 \n(79%)\n\n216/259 \n(83.4%)\n196/257 (76.3%)\n\nNorth America\n121/158 \n(76.6%)\n128/158 (81%)\n102/158 (64.6%)\n\nLatin America\n4/5 (80%)\n3/4 (75%)\n6/8 (75%)\nEurope\n124/161 \n(77.0%)\n\n147/174 \n(84.5%)\n121/161 (75.2%)\n\nAsia-Pacific\n45/54 \n(83.3%)\n48/59 (81.4%)\n53/63 (84.1%)\n\nMiscellaneous\n\nHx. of CDI in Prior \n6 months\n\n91/110 \n(82.7%)\n92/113 (81.4%)\n69/104 (66.4%)\n\nHx. of CDI in Prior \n3 months\n\n79/96 \n(82.3%)\n78/98 (79.6%)\n64/94 (68.1%)\n\nZar Score ≥2 \n45/65 \n(69.2%)\n34/55 (61.8%)\n50/80 (62.5%)\n\nCompromised \nImmunity\n\n36/53 \n(67.9%)\n67/82 (81.7%)\n43/75 (57.3%)\n\nVancomycin\n137/184 \n(74.5%)\n\n155/190 \n(81.6%)\n133/187 (71.1%)\n\nMetronidazole\n147/182 \n(80.8%)\n\n157/189 \n(83.1%)\n144/191 (75.4%)\n\nHypervirulent \nStrains\n\n52/71 \n(73.2%)\n39/51 (76.5%)\n30/46 (65.2%)\n\nEpidemic Strain\n101/127 \n(79.5%)\n83/102 (81.4%)\n88/116 (75.9%)\n\n        >70 kg\n129/168 \n(76.8%)\n\n140/178 \n(78.6%)\n108/165 (65.4%)\n\n59\n\nReference ID: 3989587\n\n\f\nClinical Review\nShrimant Mishra, MD, MPH (Efficacy)\nHiwot, Hiruy, MD, PhD (Safety)\nBLA 761046\nBezlotoxumab\n\n<=70kg\n165/210 \n(78.6%)\n\n186/217 \n(85.7%)\n174/225 (77.3%)\n\n>100 kg\n20/29 \n(69%)\n26/30 (86.7%)\n17/23 (73.9%)\n\n<=100kg\n274/349 \n(78.5%)\n\n300/365 \n(82.2%)\n265/367 (72.2%)\n\nHorn’s Index: Low \nor Moderate\n218/261 \n(83.5%)\n\n242/282 \n(85.8%)\n207/280 (73.9%)\n\nHorn’s Index: \nMajor or Extreme\n\n76/117 \n(65%)\n83/112 (74.1%)\n75/110 (68.2%)\n\n<=3 days of SOC\n164/208 \n(78.8%)\n\n172/212 \n(81.1%)\n162/217 (74.6%)\n5-6 days of SOC\n72/91 \n(79.1%)\n78/93 (83.9%)\n65/94 (69.2%)\n>6 Days of SOC\n13/18 \n(72.2%)\n27/32 (84.4%)\n19/27 (70.4%)\n\nTable 22: Study P002: Clinical Cure (Per Protocol)\n\nPlacebo\nN= 303\n(n)(%)\n\nMK-6072\nN= 331\n(n)(%)\n\nMK-3415A\nN= 337\n(n)(%) \n\nDiff. between \nPlacebo and \nMK-6072 \n(95% CI)\nClinical Cure\n252 (83.2%)\n285 (86.1%)\n253 (75.1%)\n-2.9% (-2.8, \n8.5)\n\nGlobal Cure\n\nGiven the above findings for recurrence and initial cure, it is instructive to look at the Global \nCure which accounts for both of these parameters (using the expanded definition for recurrence \nand the sponsor definition for clinical cure).  The following table highlights those findings.\n\nTable 23: Study P001: Global Cure (FAS)\n\nPlacebo\nN= 395\nn (%)\n\nMK-6072\nN= 386\nn (%)\n\nMK-3415A\nN= 383\nn (%) \n\nMK-3415\nN= 232\nn (%)\n\n164 (41.5%)\n193 (50%)\n182 (47.5%)\n85 (36.6%)\n-8.5%\n\nDiff. \nbetween \nPlacebo and \nMK-6072\nGlobal Cure \n(Expanded \nRecurrence*/Clinical \n\n60\n\nReference ID: 3989587\n\n\f\nClinical Review\nShrimant Mishra, MD, MPH (Efficacy)\nHiwot, Hiruy, MD, PhD (Safety)\nBLA 761046\nBezlotoxumab\n\nCure)\n*- Expanded includes those not tested for toxin upon diarrhea recurrence and those receiving fecal transplant, immunoglobulin, or active therapy \n\nafter SOC\n\nTable 24: Study P002 Global Cure (FAS)\n\nPlacebo\nN= 378\nn (%)\n\nMK-6072\nN= 395\nn (%)\n\nMK-3415A\nN= 390\nn (%) \n\n172 (45.5%)\n217 (54.9%)\n185 (47.4%)\n-9.4%\n\nDiff. between \nPlacebo and \nMK-6072\nGlobal Cure \n(Expanded \nRecurrence*/Clinical \nCure)\n\n*- Expanded includes those not tested for toxin upon diarrhea recurrence and those receiving fecal transplant, immunoglobulin, or active therapy \n\nafter SOC\n\nIn both studies the rate of Global Cure favored MK-6072.  \n\nData Quality and Integrity – Reviewers’ Assessment \n\nThe overall data quality was acceptable from an efficacy perspective.\n\nEfficacy Results – Secondary and other relevant endpoints\n\nMany of the secondary and exploratory endpoints were included in the discussion of the primary \nendpoint because of their clinical relevance.  However it is also instructive to look at rates of \nrecurrence in those initially cured, regardless of toxin test results at the time of recurrence.  The \nfollowing table highlights those findings.\n\n61\n\nReference ID: 3989587\n\n\f\nClinical Review\nShrimant Mishra, MD, MPH (Efficacy)\nHiwot, Hiruy, MD, PhD (Safety)\nBLA 761046\nBezlotoxumab\n\nTable 25: Study P001: Recurrence Regardless of Toxin Results (FAS)\n\nPlacebo\nN= 395\nn (%)\n\nMK-6072\nN= 386\nn (%)\n\nMK-3415A\nN= 383\nn (%) \n\nMK-3415\nN= 232\nn (%)\n\nDiff. \nbetween \nPlacebo and \nMK-6072\nRecurrence\n\n211 (53.4%) \n147 (38.1%)\n148 (38.6%)\n128 (55.2%)\n15.3%\n\nTable 26: Study P002: Recurrence Regardless of toxin Results (FAS)\n\nPlacebo\nN= 378\n(n)(%)\n\nMK-6072\nN= 395\n(n)(%)\n\nMK-3415A\nN= 390\n(n)(%) \n\nDiff. between \nPlacebo and \nMK-6072\nRecurrence \n166 (43.9%)\n149 (37.7%)\n147 (37.7%)\n6.2%\n\nIn both studies MK-6072 and MK-3415A performed considerably better than placebo using this \nmore liberal definition of recurrence.\n\nMAJOR AMENDMENT\n\nTo further assess the rick-benefit profile of bezlotoxumab, a Major Amendment was requested in \norder to evaluate 1) post hoc analyses the sponsor had conducted using expanded definitions of \nclinical cure and failure 2) the severity of recurrences and 3) clinical variables associated with \nclinical failure, particularly in subjects who might be reclassified from initial clinical failure to \ninitial clinical cure in any post-hoc analyses. The materials provided in the amendment included \nadditional datasets and CRFs.\n\nTo address concerns regarding differences in initial clinical cure, the sponsor conducted a post \nhoc analysis that altered the definition of initial clinical cure in two ways: \n\n-\nThere was no limit on the duration of SOC (previously clinical failure had been \ndefined as use longer than 14 days [16 calendar days])\n-\nTwo consecutive days of < 3 stools/24 hours could occur at any point after finishing \nSOC in order to be considered a clinical cure (previously the two consecutive days \nhad to occur immediately after the completion of SOC in order to be considered a \nclinical cure).\nThe expanded cure was assessed at various time points relative to start of infusion (2, 3, 4, 6, 8 \nand 12 weeks).\n\nClinical cure rates using the expanded definition are presented in the table below.\n\n62\n\nReference ID: 3989587\n\n\f\nClinical Review\nShrimant Mishra, MD, MPH (Efficacy)\nHiwot, Hiruy, MD, PhD (Safety)\nBLA 761046\nBezlotoxumab\n\nTable 27: Clinical Cure Rates Using Post Hoc Sponsor Definition of Clinical Cure, P001 \nand P002, FAS\n\nP001\nP002\n\n \nBezlotoxumab\nPlacebo\nBezlotoxumab\nPlacebo\n\n \nN=386\nN=386\nN=395\nN=378\n\nOriginal Definition\n299 (77.5)\n327 (82.8)\n326 (82.5)\n294 (77.8)\n\nExpanded Definition\n   2 weeks\n   3 weeks\n   4 weeks\n   6 weeks\n   8 weeks\n   12 weeks\n\n297 (76.9)\n353 (91.5)\n364 (94.3)\n370 (95.9)\n371 (96.1)\n373 (96.6)\n\n313 (79.2)\n361 (91.4)\n370 (93.7)\n375 (94.9)\n377 (95.4)\n379 (95.9)\n\n308 (78.0)\n367 (92.9)\n372 (94.2)\n376 (95.2)\n377 (95.4)\n377 (95.4)\n\n296 (78.3)\n340 (89.9)\n349 (92.3)\n353 (93.4)\n354 (93.7)\n356 (94.2)\n\nAs can be noted, by 2 weeks, differences in clinical cure between the two arms start to reduce \nand are gone by 3 weeks.\nTo better understand whether differences in clinical cures under the original clinical cure \ndefinition were possibly related to strict definition criteria, which may not be clinically relevant \nand whether expanded definition of clinical cure was clinically acceptable, clinical \ncharacteristics of subjects reclassified as cures were analyzed, see the table below. \n\n63\n\nReference ID: 3989587\n\n\f\nClinical Review\nShrimant Mishra, MD, MPH (Efficacy)\nHiwot, Hiruy, MD, PhD (Safety)\nBLA 761046\nBezlotoxumab\n\nTable 28: Characteristics of Initial CDI Episode in Subjects Reclassified from Clinical \nFailure to Clinical Cure Using Post Hoc Sponsor Definition of Clinical Cure, P001 and \nP002\n\n74 Subjects\n52 Subjects\n51 Subjects\n61 Subjects\n\nP001\nP002\nMK-6072\nPlacebo\nMK-6072\nPlacebo\nNumber \nReclassified\n\n16 days\n(10.5-19)\n\n16 days \n(12.25-21.75)\n\n17 days\n(15-21)\n\n16 days\n(13.75-20)\n\nMedian Duration \nof SOC \n(25%-75%)\n\n3 days\n(2-5.25)\n\n3 days\n(2-7.75)\n\n3 days\n(2-5)\n\n3.5 days\n(2-6)\n\nMedian Duration \nfrom End of \nSOC to \nResolution of \nDiarrhea\n(25%-75%)\n\n15 subjects\n15 subjects\n20 subjects\n21 subjects\n\nNumber with \nRecurrences\n\nLooking at the reclassified bezlotoxumab subjects, their clinical course did not look particularly \nsevere relative to the original definition of clinical cure and failure.  For example, a subject could \nhave taken 16 calendar days of SOC under the original definition for clinical cure. Similarly, \nunder the expanded definition in the bezlotoxumab arm the median duration of SOC treatment is \n16-17 days.  Similarly, under the original definition, a cured subject would be classified as a cure \nif he/she had no diarrhea for the two days immediately following the last day of SOC(i.e. - a \nduration of 2 days from the end of SOC to the defined resolution of diarrhea).  The median \nduration for the reclassified bezlotxumab subjects was 3 days, which implied that relative to the \noriginal definition, subjects had an extra day of diarrhea following the last day of SOC.  Thus, \nfrom this descriptive analysis there are signals that some of the original clinical failures may not \nhave been particularly clinically severe.   \n\nExploration of the CRF’s of a subset of reclassified bezlotoxumab subjects in P001 appeared to \nsupport these analyses in that it was difficult to pinpoint in the majority of cases a direct \nrelationship between receipt of bezlotoxumab and clinical failure.  Cases were often confounded, \nsuch as in cases where a subject may have received short durations of SOC.  If a link was \nplausible between drug and clinical failure it often caused only a modest prolonging of the initial \nillness.  \n\nWith the use of the expanded definition of clinical cure, the number of clinical cures in all arms \nexpanded, and thus the number of subjects that could be evaluated for recurrences also expanded.  \nUsing this definition of clinical cure and the original definition for recurrences, the number of \n\n64\n\nReference ID: 3989587\n\n\f\nClinical Review\nShrimant Mishra, MD, MPH (Efficacy)\nHiwot, Hiruy, MD, PhD (Safety)\nBLA 761046\nBezlotoxumab\n\nrecurrences was as noted in the table below.\n\nTable 29: Recurrences Using Post Hoc Sponsor Definition of Clinical Cure, FAS, Study \nP001\n\nMK-6072\nN= 386\n\nPlacebo\nN=395\n\nDifference \n(Placebo – MK6072)\n\n82 (21.2%)\n124 (31.4%)\n10.2%\n\nRecurrences at 12 \nWeeks\n\nTable 30: Recurrences Using Post Hoc Sponsor Definition of Clinical Cure, FAS, Study \nP002\n\nMK-6072\nN= 395\n\nPlacebo\nN=378\n\nDifference \n(Placebo – MK6072)\n\n82 (20.8%)\n118 (31.2%)\n10.4%\n\nRecurrences at 12 \nWeeks\n\nAs can be seen, using this altered clinical cure definition, a reduction in recurrences can still be \nnoted with bezlotoxumab in both studies\n\nThese recurrences were evaluated in terms of severity based on multiple parameters including \nneed for/type of treatment, need/location of hospitalization, and clinical characteristics. Note the \ntable below (note that this table focuses on 1st recurrences).\n\nTable 31: Clinical Characteristics of 1st Recurrences Using Post Hoc Sponsor Definition of \nClinical Cure, Study P001\n\nRecurrences at 12 \nweeks\n\nMK-6072\nN=82\n\nPlacebo\nN= 124\n\n28 (34.2%)\n40 (32.3%)\n-1.9%\n\nDifference\n(Placebo-MK6072)\nTreatment in health \ncare facility\n\n1 day\n\nICU Admission\n4 (4.9%)\n3 (2.4%)\n-2.5%\nAny SOC use\n46 (56.1%)\n76 (61.3%)\n5.2%\n     Vancomycin \n30 (36.6%)\n59 (47.6%)\n11.0%\n     Metronidazole\n13 (15.8%)\n21 (16.9%)\n1.1%\n     Fidaxomicin\n4 (4.9%)\n5 (4.0%)\n-0.9%\nMean Duration of \nSOC, Days (Range)\n\n11 \n(0-63)\n\n12 \n(0-60)\n\nZar Score ≥ 2\n4 (4.9%)\n12 (9.7%)\n4.8%\nWBC > 15,000 \n1 (1.2%)\n7 (5.6%)\n4.4%\n\n65\n\nReference ID: 3989587\n\n\f\nClinical Review\nShrimant Mishra, MD, MPH (Efficacy)\nHiwot, Hiruy, MD, PhD (Safety)\nBLA 761046\nBezlotoxumab\n\n1 stool\n\n3\n(3-4)\n\n4\n(3-6)\n\ncells/µL\nTemperature > 38.3°C\n1 (1.2%)\n3 (2.4%)\n1.2%\nMedian Number of \nLoose Stools at Onset \n(25-75%)\n\n1 day\n\n2\n(1-4)\n\n3\n(1-5)\n\nMedian Time to \nResolution of \nRecurrence, Days (25-\n75%)\n\n3 (3.7%)\n6 (4.8%)\n1.1%\n\nEventual Need for \nFecal Transplant\n\nTable 32: Clinical Characteristics of 1st Recurrences Using Post Hoc Sponsor Definition of \nClinical Cure, P002\n\nRecurrences at 12 \nweeks\n\nMK-6072\nN=82\n\nPlacebo\nN= 118\n\n30 (36.6%)\n41 (34.8%)\n-1.8%\n\nDifference\n(Placebo-MK6072)\nTreatment in health \ncare facility\n\n2 days\n\nICU Admission\n4 (4.9%)\n3 (2.5%)\n-2.4%\nAny SOC use\n45 (54.9%)\n77 (65.3%)\n10.4%\n     Vancomycin \n30 (36.6%)\n60 (50.8%)\n14.2%\n     Metronidazole\n21 (25.6%)\n24 (20.3%)\n-5.3%\n     Fidaxomicin\n5 (6.1%)\n5 (4.2%)\n-1.9%\nMean Duration of \nSOC, Days (Range)\n\n10 \n(0-52)\n\n12 \n(0-73)\n\n1 (1.2%)\n12 (10.2%)\n9.0%\n\nZar Score ≥ 2\n7 (8.5%)\n13 (11%)\n2.5%\nWBC > 15,000 \ncells/µL\n\n0 stools\n\n4\n(3-5)\n\n4\n(3-6)\n\nTemperature > 38.3°C\n1 (1.2%)\n3 (2.5%)\n1.3%\nMedian Number of \nLoose Stools at Onset \n(25-75%)\n\n1 day\n\n2\n(1-5)\n\n3\n(1-4)\n\nMedian Time to \nResolution of \nRecurrence, Days (25-\n75%)\n\n2 (2.4%)\n16 (13.6%)\n11.2%\n\nEventual Need for \nFecal Transplant\n\nOverall, clinical implications of CDI recurrences were not trivial indicating clinical benefit of \n\n66\n\nReference ID: 3989587\n\n\f\nClinical Review\n\nShrimant Mishra, MD, MPH (Efficacy)\nHiwot, Hiruy, MD, PhD (Safety)\n\nBLA 761046\n\nBezlotoxumab\n\npreventing recurrences. More than a half of the patients required a repeat course of SOC and\nabout a third of the patients required hospitalization including ICU admission in 2.5-5% of cases.\n\nThough not particularly striking for all parameters, in POO1 trends toward less severity were\nnoted in the bezlotoxumab arm, particularly with regards to need for treatment. Similarly, in\nP002, trends toward less severity were noted in the bezlotoxumab arm in terms of need for\ntreatment and fecal transplant.\n\nDose/Dose Response\n\nDose response was not studied in the Phase 2 or 3 trials. All subjects received the 10 mg/kg dose\nof study drug in these trials.\n\nDurability of Response\n\nAs has already been discussed, both studies P001 and P002 assessed for recurrences over a\nperiod of 12 weeks. However, in P002, a subset of 300 subjects were followed for an additional\nnine months (the extension cohort). 293 of these subjects were a part of the FAS (112 subjects in\nthe MK-3415A arm, 99 in the MK-6072 arm, and 82 in the placebo arm). When looking at\nglobal cures over the 12 month period in the extension cohort, the MK-6072 arm did\nconsiderably better than placebo (69.7% global cures vs. 40.2%, respectively). However, the\nextension cohort appeared to differ significantly from the full P0O2 FAS. In fact the rate of\nrecurrence over the 12 week study period in the full FAS set in the placebo arm was 25.7% while\nit was 41.5% over the same period for the placebo arm of the extension cohort (using the sponsor\ndefinition for recurrence). Thus, it is difficult to draw any real conclusions regarding durability\ngiven the atypical characteristics of the extension cohort.\n\nPersistence of Effect\n\nSee above discussion regarding durability of response.\n\n7 Integrated Review of Effectiveness\n\n7A, Assessment of Efficacy Across Trials\n\n7.1.1. Primary Endpoint\n\n7.1.2. Secondary and Other Endpoints\n\nBecause of the disparate findings across the individual trials, particularly as regards clinical cure,\n67\n\nReference ID: 3989587\n\n\f\nClinical Review\n\nShrimant Mishra, MD, MPH (Efficacy)\nHiwot, Hiruy, MD, PhD (Safety)\n\nBLA 761046\n\nBezlotoxumab\n\npooling for analysis of the primary and secondary endpoint is not considered appropriate.\nMoreover, studies P001 and P002 had some differences as regards racial makeup as well as\nlocation of investigative sites.\n\n7.1.3. Subpopulations\n\nBecause of the disparate findings across the individual trials, the differences in study design as\nregards study sites and racial makeup, pooling results is not appropriate in this setting.\n\n7.1.4. Dose and Dose-Response\n\nOnly a single dose (10mg/kg) was studied in the phase 2 and phase 3 trials, thus dose response in\nthe clinical setting cannot be adequately evaluated. The sponsor chose this dose because the\nexposure it afforded was similar to the protective exposure noted in hamster models of CDI. The\nsponsor notes that higher doses were not studied because it was felt that they would provide no\nincreased benefit. Lower doses were not studied because the 10mg/kg dose was sufficiently\ntolerated in phase | and 2 studies,\n\n7.1.5. Onset, Duration, and Durability of Efficacy Effects\n\nThe primary endpoint covered a 12 week period. Please see earlier discussions regarding\nefficacy.\n\n7.2. Additional Efficacy Considerations\n7.2.1. Considerations on Benefit in the Postmarket Setting\n[See discussion in Section 7.3]\n7.2.2. Other Relevant Benefits\nSee discussion in Section 7.3\n73. Integrated Assessment of Effectiveness\n\nIn assessing the efficacy of MK-6072 in preventing the recurrence of CDI from the data\npresented in this review, it is helpful to methodically move through the salient issues.\n\nFirst, what is the clinical impact of any therapy designed to reduce recurrence of CDI?\nAssuming that the therapy had no significant deleterious effects on clinical cure of the initial\nepisode and had a tolerable safety profile, the clinical impact would be significant. Currently\nthere is no approved therapy to treat the recurrence of CDI. Instead, clinicians have applied a\nheterogeneous group of unapproved therapies to treat recurrences. These range from repeating\nthe intimal treatment course to pulsed vancomycin tapers to immunoglobulin therapy to fecal\n\nReference ID: 3989587\n\n68\n\n\f\nClinical Review\nShrimant Mishra, MD, MPH (Efficacy)\nHiwot, Hiruy, MD, PhD (Safety)\nBLA 761046\nBezlotoxumab\n\ntransplant. Reductions in recurrences would have a significant impact on patient morbidity and \nhealth care costs directly and indirectly.  Thus, there is no question that the clinical impact of an \napproved therapy to prevent recurrence would be significant.\n\nSecondly, it is important to consider how clinicians might use any therapy directed toward \npreventing recurrence.  In an ideal scenario, any adjunct therapy to prevent recurrence would be \ngiven at the time of/right after initial clinical cure.  This would spare the clinician and patient the \nrisk and cost associated with administering the adjunct therapy early in the course of the initial \nCDAD episode.  Such risk would include administering adjunct therapy to a subject that was \nnever cured initially (in which case preventing recurrence becomes moot) or administering \ntherapy that might somehow actually interfere with the SOC treatment of the initial episode.  \n\nBearing the above considerations in mind, one can now evaluate the data submitted in this \napplication.\n\nIn regards to recurrences, no matter what definitions of recurrence or clinical cure was used (the \noriginal applicant definitions of recurrence  and cure, the reviewer expanded definition of \nrecurrence, or the applicant expanded definition of clinical cure), bezlotoxumab appeared to \neither have a statistically significant effect or a trend toward lessening recurrences. \n\nUsing the reviewer expanded definition of recurrence that captures subjects not tested for toxin \nupon recurrence of diarrhea as well as those who used therapies that could essentially treat CDI, \nMK-6072 appears to perform well relative to placebo in P001 and marginally better in study \nP002. In P001, MK-6072 had an absolute reduction in recurrence rate of 13.6%. Moreover, MK-\n6072 appeared to do better than placebo in preventing recurrence in almost all subgroups tested.  \nIn study P002, MK-6072 had absolute reduction in recurrence rate of 5.3%. Though this \nreduction was modest, the MK-6072 arm had lower recurrence rates in almost every subgroup \n(except, importantly, in males). \n\nIt is interesting to note that in P001 administration of SOC for ≤ 3 days before study drug \ninfusion was associated with “less” favorable outcomes, and in P002, administration of SOC for \n5-6 days before study drug infusion was associated with “more” favorable outcomes.  Whether \nthis is reflective of an overall improved activity of MK-6072 on recurrence when given later in \nthe initial course of SOC is unclear, particularly given that the opposite effect was seen in \nsubjects who had received >6 days of SOC before receiving study drug infusion (though the \nsample  size in this subgroup is quite small).  It should be noted that the original recurrence \ndefinition did not distinguish between those requiring treatment and those who did not.  \nHowever, there appeared to be a trend toward less need for treatment in the bezlotoxumab arm; \nthis was in conjunction with other exploratory analyses that seemed to suggest some decreased \nseverity of recurrence with bezlotoxumab. Overall, there appears to be a modest to moderate \neffect of MK-6072 on recurrence.\n\n69\n\nReference ID: 3989587\n\n\f\nClinical Review\nShrimant Mishra, MD, MPH (Efficacy)\nHiwot, Hiruy, MD, PhD (Safety)\nBLA 761046\nBezlotoxumab\n\nIn regards to clinical cure, MK-6072 appeared to perform more poorly relative to placebo in \nstudy P001 with an absolute reduction in clinical cure of 5.3%. MK-6072 also performed worse \nin virtually every subgroup analyzed in this study. In study P002, MK-6072 had the opposite \neffect with an absolute increase in the rate of clinical cure of 4.7%.  There were, however, \nseveral subgroups where placebo performed numerically better than MK-6072. In terms of the \nduration of SOC prior to receiving study drug infusion, no clear trend can be seen from the two \ntrials (although in subjects >6 days of SOC there was trend toward better clinical cures in the \nMK-6072 arm; again, the sample size was quite small).  Thus, MK-6072’s effect on initial \nclinical cure is unclear. However, in exploratory analyses using the expanded definition of \nclinical cure, the rates of clinical cure in study arms are similar and it appears that many of \nclinical failures defined according to the original cure definition were only modestly clinically \nsignificant. A subset review of CRFs of reclassified cures from P001 did not provide any clear \nlink between administration of bezlotoxumab and initial clinical failure.  In totality, \nbezlotoxumab may have a negative effect on clinical cure, but it appears to be clinically minor \nand such risk may be tolerable in high benefit scenarios. \n\nOverall, when looking at the effect of MK-6072 on the two most important clinical parameters, \nrecurrence and initial clinical cure, a positive risk benefit calculus seems plausible.  There is \ncertainly a level of uncertainty when relying on stool diaries, etc. but hopefully in this blinded \ntrial those uncertainties are minimized. . In general, there appears to be a moderate reduction in \nrecurrences at the possible expense of small increases in mostly minor initial clinical failures. \nStill, this would place the significant risk-benefit calculus involved in deciding whether or not to \nprescribe the study drug to a patient with CDI at the feet of clinicians, when ideally this process \nshould have been definitively made much earlier- i.e.-during this drug development and \nregulatory review process. \n\nIt is imperative that clinicians use the drug judiciously, hopefully reserving its use for those \nsubjects with significant risk factors for recurrence.  Given the likely cost of this medication, \nsuch decision-making is even more imperative.  Ideally, proper guidance regarding the timing of \nstudy drug relative to SOC would have been delineated from the trial results but given the \nheterogeneous nature of drug receipt relative to SOC as well as the heterogeneous results, no \nclear recommendation can be made with regard to timing of bezlotoxumab.\nThe Division and Applicant may consider a PMR/PMC for a study evaluating the timing of study \ndrug administration relative to SOC in regards to effects on both clinical cure and recurrence.  \nSuch a study should have a mutually agreed upon definition of clinical cure and recurrence and \ncould stratify subjects based on timing of SOC. \n\n70\n\nReference ID: 3989587\n\n\f\nClinical Review\n\nShrimant Mishra, MD, MPH (Efficacy)\nHiwot, Hiruy, MD, PhD (Safety)\n\nBLA 761046\n\nBezlotoxumab\n\n8 Review of Safety\n\n8.1. Safety Review Approach\n\nThis safety review focuses on data from the safety population enrolled in the two Phase 3\nrandomized, placebo-controlled trials, study P00O1 and P002, supporting this application. These\ntwo studies evaluated the efficacy, safety and tolerability of a single infusion of bezlotoxumab\n(bezlo) alone and in combination with actoxumab (acto) for prevention of Clostridium difficile\ninfection (CDI) in patients receiving standard of care antibacterial therapy (oral vancomycin,\nfidaxomicin, oral or intravenous metronidazole). Both bezlo and acto are fully human\nmonoclonal antibodies: bezlo is an anti- type B Clostridium difficile toxin antibody and acto is an\nanti-type A Clostridium difficile toxin antibody.\n\nThe two studies are similar in design with the following exceptions: the first study, study P001,\nfollowed an adaptive design. It also had four arms study arms: subjects that received acto\ninfusion only (acto arm), subjects that received bezlo infusion alone (bezlo arm), subjects that\nreceived combination of acto and bezlo infusions (acto+bezlo arm), and subjects that received\nnormal saline infusion (placebo arm). In addition, study P001 had a planned interim analysis at\n40% enrollment to evaluate each study arm and to drop one of the single therapy arms if it did\nnot demonstrate efficacy more than the combination arm. Based on the interim analysis, which\nshowed worsening outcome in the acto arm as compared to all three arms, the acto arm was\ndropped for the post-interim analysis study period. Study P002 did not have planned interim\nanalysis and was designed with only 3 arms: bezlo arm, acto+bezlo arm and placebo arm. In\naddition, study P002 had an extended safety cohort of 300 participants that were followed for\nadditional 9 months after the 12-week study period. Given the similarity in study design,\nincluding adverse event (AE) reporting period, pooled data from study P001 and P002 are\npresented.\n\nThe safety population included subjects in Study P001 and P002 that received partial or full\ninfusion of bezlo, acto+bezlo or placebo. Of note, subjects that received acto alone were\nexcluded from the safety population. However, data from the acto arm is presented separately for\ncompleteness of the review since the safety population includes subjects that received the\ncombined acto+bezlo infusion. Also, data from a phase 2 study that compared the combination of\nacto and bezlo with placebo, study P017, is not pooled with the phase 3 studies mentioned above\ndue to difference in AE reporting time frame The safety data for this study is presented\nseparately.\n\nSince bezlo is a monoclonal antibody, special attention was given to adverse events related to\nimmunogenicity, and infusion-related adverse reactions.\n\nReference ID: 3989587\n\n71\n\n\f\nClinical Review\n\nShrimant Mishra, MD, MPH (Efficacy)\nHiwot, Hiruy, MD, PhD (Safety)\n\nBLA 761046\n\nBezlotoxumab\n\nFor the integrated safety analysis, MAED version 18.0, JMP version 12.0 and JReview 9.2\nanalytic software were utilized.\n\n8.2. Review of the Safety Database\n\n8.2.1. Overall Exposure\n\nOverall, there were 1790 subjects exposed to bezlo either alone or in combination with acto; 126\nwere healthy subjects in Phase 1 studies (30 subjects received bezlo alone and 96 received acto +\nbezlo), and 1664 were subjects in Phase 2 and 3 (786 received bezlotoxumab alone and 878\nreceived actot+bezlo). Of these, 1741 subjects were exposed to bezlo dose at 10mg/kg, while 30\nand 19 were exposed to dose of <10mg/kg dose and 20mg/kg, respectively. With the exception\nof subjects in one phase | study (study P004) who received two doses of acto+bezlo (n=30), all\nsubjects were exposed to a single dose of bezlo or actotbezlo.\n\nMajority of subjects in the two Phase 3 trials that make up the safety population received bezlo\nalone or in combination with acto at the intended bezlo dose of 10mg/kg, with intended dose\ndefined as >9.5 to <10.5 mg/kg. Of the 786 that received bezlo alone, 97.1% (n=763) received\nthe intended bezlo dose. Only 1.8% (n=9) subjects received less than the intended dose (defined\nas <9.5 mg/kg) and 1.1% (n=9) received more than the intended dose (defined as >10.5 mg/kg).\nNo subjects received bezlo at a dose >20 mg/kg. Among the 777 that received acto+bezlo, 96.7%\n(n=751) received the intended dose of 10mg/kg, and 2.3% (n=18) and 1 %( n=8) received less\nthan and more than the intended dose, respectively.\n\nSince bezlo is dosed on a milligram per kilogram(mg/kg) basis, the range of doses was large\ngiven the range of subject weights (range of weights 29.8 to 194 kg; range of doses about 300 to\n2000 mg). The median dose was 700 mg, consistent with the median weight of 70 kg among\nbezlo-treated subjects.\n\nTable 33 summarizes the overall extent of exposure to bezlo throughout the drug development\nprogram.\n\nTable 33: Bezlotoxumab-exposed Population during Development Program\n\nSafety Database for Bezlotoxumab\nIndividuals exposed to the study drug in this development program for the indication\n\nunder review\nN=1790\nNew Drug Placebo\nClinical Trial Groups (n=1790 ) (n= 892 )\nPhase 1 Studies (Healthy Subjects)\n\nReference ID: 3989587\n\n72\n\n\f\nClinical Review\n\nShrimant Mishra, MD, MPH (Efficacy)\nHiwot, Hiruy, MD, PhD (Safety)\n\nBLA 761046\n\nBezlotoxumab\n\nP020 54 0\nbezlo\n\n<10mg/kg | 18\n\n10mg/kg | 6\n\n>10mg/kg | 6\nactotbezlo\n\n<10mg/kg | 12\n\n10mg/kg | 6\n\n>10mg/kg | 6\nP005 29 6\nactot+ bezlo(10mg/kg) 29\nP006 13 6\nactotbezlo(10mg/kg) 6\nactot+bezlo(20mg/kg) 7\nP004 30 0\nactot+bezlo(10mg/kg)-two doses 30\nControlled trials conducted for this\nindication\nPhase 2-Study P017 101 99\nactot+bezlo (10mg/kg) 101 99\nPhase 3-Study P001 777 400\nbezlo (10mg/kg) 390\nacto+bezlo (10mg/kg) 387\nPhase 3-Study P002 786 381\nbezlo (10mg/kg) 396\nactotbezlo (10mg/kg) 390\n\nbezlo: bezlotoxumab; actot+bezlo: Combination of actoxumab and bezlotoxumab\nNote: Subjects in the acto+bezlo arm received the stated dose for each immunoglobulin.\n\n8.2.2. Relevant characteristics of the safety population:\n\nThe safety population is comprised of all subjects in the two phase 3 studies that received partial\nor full infusion of the study medication. The subjects that received the same study treatment in\nthe two Phase 3 trials, i.e. acto+bezlo, bezlotoxumab, or placebo are pooled. For the safety\nanalysis, subjects were grouped in to the treatment arm they received (as treated), as opposed to\nthe group they were randomized to. There were total of 7 subjects that received treatments\n\nReference ID: 3989587\n\n73\n\n\f\nClinical Review\nShrimant Mishra, MD, MPH (Efficacy)\nHiwot, Hiruy, MD, PhD (Safety)\nBLA 761046\nBezlotoxumab\n\ndifferent from their randomized arm. In study P001, two subjects randomized to bezlo received \nplacebo, and one subject randomized to acto+bezlo received placebo. In study P002, one subject \nrandomized to bezlo received placebo; a second subject randomized to placebo received \nacto+bezlo; a third subject randomized to acto+bezlo received bezlo alone, and a fourth subject \nrandomized to acto+bezlo received acto alone. \n\nOf note, the safety population did not include subjects that received acto alone in study P001. \nThe data from the acto only arm are included in the tables for comparison only. \n\nTables 34, 35 and 36 summarize the relevant baseline demographic, clinical and laboratory \ncharacteristics of the pooled safety population, respectively.\n\nTable 34: Demographic Characteristics of Safety Population\n\nBezlo\n\nPlacebo\n\nTotal\n\nActo*\n\nN, %\n\nActo+\nBezlo      \nN, %\n\nN, %\n\nN, %\n\nN, %\n\n5 (0.6)\n2 (0.3)\n4 (0.5)\n11 (0.5)\n0\n\nSubjects in population\n786\n777\n781\n2344\n236\nGender\nMale\n343 (43.6)\n348 (44.8)\n326 (41.7)\n1017 (43.4)\n103 (43.6)\nFemale\n443 (56.4)\n429 (55.2)\n455 (58.3)\n1327 (56.6)\n133 (56.4)\nAge (Years)\nMedian\n62\n64.2\n63.5\n63.2\n66\nRange\n18-100\n18-95\n18-98\n18-100\n21-99\n<40\n105 (13.4)\n85 (11)\n85 (10.8)\n275 (11.8)\n21 (8.9)\n40-59\n211 (26.9)\n186 (23.9)\n199 (25.4)\n596 (25.5)\n67 (28.4)\n60-79\n327 (41.6)\n343 (44.2)\n344 (44.1)\n1014 (43.2)\n103 (43.6)\n≥80\n143 (18.6)\n183 (21)\n153 (19.6)\n459(19.6)\n45 (19.1)\nRace\nAmerican Indian or Alaska \nNative\n\nAsian\n68 (8.7)\n70 (9)\n59 (7.6)\n197 (8.4)\n3 (1.3)\nBlack or African American\n46 (5.9)\n35 (4.5)\n28 (3.6)\n109 (4.7)\n16 (6.8)\nMultiple\n15 (1.9)\n12 (1.5)\n8 (1)\n35 (1.5)\n3 (1.3)\nWhite\n652 (83)\n658 (84.7)\n682 (87.3)\n1992 (85)\n214 (90.4)\nEthnicity\nHispanic or Latino\n79 (10.1)\n86 (11.1)\n103 (13.2)\n268 (11.4)\n23 (9.7)\nNon-Hispanic or Latino\n683 (86.9)\n668 (86)\n655 (83.9)\n2006 (85.6)\n211 (89.4)\nNot Re