 (76.7)\nRenal disease4\nSevere\n52 (6.6)\n39 (5)\n52 (6.7)\n143 (6.1)\n11 (4.7)\n\n75\n\nReference ID: 3989587\n\n\f\nClinical Review\n\nShrimant Mishra, MD, MPH (Efficacy)\nHiwot, Hiruy, MD, PhD (Safety)\n\nBLA 761046\nBezlotoxumab\n\nBezlo Actot Placebo Total Acto\nBezlo Only\nN,% N, % N,% N,%\nSubjects in population 786 777 781 2344 236\nModerate 46 (5.9) 42 (5.4) 47 (6) 135(5.8) 12 (5.1)\nNo or mild 688 (87.5) | 696 (89.6) | 682 (87.3) | 2066 (88.1) | 213 (90.3)\n\nICDI was classified as severe if subjects score >2points based on the following criteria: Age >60\nyears (1 point), body temperature >38.3°C (1 point), serum albumin<2.5mg/dL (1 point),\nperipheral white blood cell count >15000 cells/mm? within 48 hours (1 point), endoscopic\n\nevidence of pseudomembranous colitis (2 points), and treatment in ICU (2 points).\n\n?Charlson index (Charlson et al, J. Chron Dis 1987) was used to assess co-morbid conditions\n3SoC tx refers to standard of care treatment subjects received in conjunction with study drug\n\n4 Renal disease is classified as severe if subjects had one of the three conditions: uremia, on\ndialysis or have had transplant. Mild and moderate are classified with serum creatinine 2-3mg/dL\n\nand >3mg/dL respectively.\n\nNOTE: Subjects in acto arm are NOT included in the safety population; data from this arm is\npresented for comparison only.\n\nTable 36: Key Baseline Laboratory Features of Safety Population\n\nBezlo Actot+ Placebo Total Acto\nBezlo Only\nN, % N, % N, % N, % N, %\nSubjects in population 786 7717 781 2344 236\nNAP1/027 strain, N(%)\nYes 90(11.5) 76(9.8) 100(12.8) | 290(12.4) | 2410.2)\nNo 394(50.1) | 401(51.6) | 390(49.9) | 1305(55.7) | 120(50.8)\nUnknown 302(38.4) | 300(38.6) | 291(37.3) | 985(42) 92(39)\nEpidemic strains*\nYes 211 222(28.6) | 233 723 57(24.1)\nNo 273 255(32.8) | 257 872 87(36.9)\nUnknown 302 300(38.6) | 291 985 92(39)\nBaseline WBC\n>15x107/uL\nYes 57 (7.3) 68 (8.8) 60 (7.7) 185 (7.9) | 14 (5.9)\nNo 689 (87.7) | 667 ( 669 (85.7 | 2025 204 (86.4)\n85.8) (86.4)\nUnknown 40 (5) 42 (5.4) 52 (6.6) 134 (5.7) _| 18 (7.6)\n\n“Epidemic strains included NAP1/027, 001, 0078 and106 strains of C. difficile\n\n8.2.3. Adequacy of the safety database:\n\nReference ID: 3989587\n\n76\n\n\f\nClinical Review\n\nShrimant Mishra, MD, MPH (Efficacy)\nHiwot, Hiruy, MD, PhD (Safety)\n\nBLA 761046\n\nBezlotoxumab\n\nOverall, the safety database provided adequate numbers of exposed subjects at the proposed dose\nand frequency (one time use) for bezlo. Most of the baseline demographic, prior history of CDI,\ncomorbidity, disease severity and laboratory features appear comparable among the three arms.\nMore than half of the subjects in each arm were older than 60 years of age, which reflects CDI’s\nclinical burden in the elderly. The range of severity of disease in the safety population represents\nthe severity range encountered in clinical practice. Of note, there is underrepresentation of\nminority populations as subjects of white racial background accounted for >80% of subjects in\neach arm. There is some literature evidence of higher incidence of CDI in white vs non-white\nindividuals, but there is no major difference in manifestation of CDI across racial groups. Hence,\nthe information obtained from these studies could be generalized to the greater US population.\n\n8.3. Adequacy of Applicant’s clinical Safety Assessment\n\n8.3.1. Issues Regarding Data Integrity and Submission Quality\n\nIn collaboration with the Office of Computational Science (OCS), data fitness assessment was\nperformed on January 14, 2016. The result showed some inconsistencies in the data. For\nexample, the death information, particularly timing, between the various domains was\ninconsistent with some fatal adverse events not having a death record in the disposition domain.\nThere were instances where subject death information was captured in the Comments (CO) or\nClinical Events (CE) domains. In addition, a full comprehensive file of all the deaths that\noccurred during the pivotal studies was not provided initially and was obtained by information\nrequest.\n\nCase report forms were reviewed to assess consistency of the data submitted. A coding\ndictionary was created to assess how AEs were transcribed to MedDRA dictionary LLT and\npreferred term. All of the reported terms for AE matched the MedDRA dictionaries used during\nthe study. There were few instances of some splitting terms. For example, the term cardiac\n\nfailure was reported as “cardiac failure”, “cardiac failure acute”, “cardiac failure chronic”, and\n“cardiac failure congestive”.\n\nOf note, clinical inspection of 4 study sites were requested from the Office of Scientific\nInvestigation (OSI), and the results of the inspections are pending at time of filing of this review.\nThe results of the clinical inspection will be amended when results are available.\n\n8.3.2. Categorization of Adverse Events\n\nThe sponsor defined AE as any unfavorable and unintended sign (including an abnormal\n\nReference ID: 3989587\n\n77\n\n\f\nClinical Review\nShrimant Mishra, MD, MPH (Efficacy)\nHiwot, Hiruy, MD, PhD (Safety)\nBLA 761046\nBezlotoxumab\n\nlaboratory finding, for example), symptom, or disease temporally associated with the use of a \nmedicinal product or protocol- specified procedure, whether or not considered related to the \nmedicinal product or protocol- specified procedure. Any worsening (i.e., any clinically \nsignificant adverse change in rate and/or intensity) of a preexisting condition that is temporally \nassociated with the use of a pharmaceutical product is also an AE. AEs that occurred after \ninfusion of study drug were classified as treatment emergent AEs (TEAEs). AEs were reported \nby System Organ Class (SOC), as specified by the Medical Dictionary for Regulatory Activities \n(MedDRA) Version 17.1 for study P001 and MedDRA Version 18.0 for study P002.  MedDRA \nVersion 18.0 was used for the pooled safety analysis.\n\nClinical AEs were recorded in case report forms over the 12-week post infusion study period \nduring visits on days 4, 11, 29, 57 and 85. Vital signs were recorded pre-infusion, 30-minutes \nafter start of infusion, end of infusion and during the above mentioned study visits.  \nElectrocardiogram study was done immediately prior to start of infusion and within 2-hrs after \nend of infusion. Laboratory assessments (hematology, chemistry, and urinalysis) were carried out \nduring study visits on days 1, 4, 11, and 29.  Blood samples for anti-drug antibody were collected \nduring study visit days 1, 29, 57 and 85. \n\nAEs were assessed by frequency, with each event per patient reported.   AE severity was \ncategorized into mild, moderate, and severe. In addition, a serious AE (SAE) was defined as any \nAE occurring at any dose that:\n\n• Results in death\n• Is life-threatening\n• Results in persistent or significant disability/incapacity\n• Results in or prolongs an existing inpatient hospitalization\n• Is a congenital anomaly/birth defect\n• Is a cancer\n• Is associated with an overdose\n• Is another important medical event\n\nIn the two Phase 3 trials (P001, P002), non-serious AEs were collected from the time of study \nmedication infusion until Week 4 post-infusion. Serious adverse events (SAEs) were collected \nfrom the time of study medication infusion until the Week 12 post-infusion visit. The study \ninvestigator assessed the duration, intensity and relationship of the AE to the study drug. In the \n9-month extension for P002, only deaths and SAEs deemed treatment-related by the investigator \nwere reported.\n\nSince bezlo is a monoclonal antibody, the sponsor carried out AE monitoring specifically for \ninfusion-specific reactions by selectively looking at AEs that occurred within the 24-hour post \ninfusion of the study drug. The sponsor looked at the following specific terms to assess clinical \nevents that may be related to infusion reaction: infusion-site adverse experience, pyrexia, chills, \nrash, arthralgia, myalgia, joint swelling, obstructive airways disorder, bronchospasm, stridor, \ndysphonia, headache, fatigue, pruritus, urticaria, hypotension, hypertension, nasal congestion, \n\n78\n\nReference ID: 3989587\n\n\f\nClinical Review\n\nShrimant Mishra, MD, MPH (Efficacy)\nHiwot, Hiruy, MD, PhD (Safety)\n\nBLA 761046\n\nBezlotoxumab\n\nnausea, vomiting, flushing, angioedema, dyspnea, and dizziness/lightheadedness. The\ninformation was collected for inpatient subjects while outpatient subjects, who made up about a\nthird of the study population, were instructed to call the study staff if AEs occurred within the\nfirst 24 hours post infusion.\n\n8.3.3. Routine Clinical Tests\n\nThe schedule of events for study P002 is presented in table 37 as an example for the timing of\nsafety lab collection in the safety population. Similar laboratory testing schedule was employed\nin study P001.\n\nReference ID: 3989587\n\n79\n\n\f\nClinical Review\nShrimant Mishra, MD, MPH (Efficacy)\nHiwot, Hiruy, MD, PhD (Safety)\nBLA 761046\nBezlotoxumab\n\nTable 37: Study P002 Schedule of Clinical and Safety Evaluation\n\nActivity\nTime \nPoint\n\nStudy Visit\n#1\n#2\n#\n3\n#4\n#5\n#6\nUnscheduled\n\nWeek  12 \n\nWeek  8\n(Day 57 ± 7 \n\n(Day 85 ± 5 \n\nRelative Day/Week of\nStudy\nDay \n1\n\nDay 4 ( ± 1 \nday)\n\nDay 11 (± 2 \ndays)\n\nWeek  \n4\n29\n3\n\nX\nX\n\nDay 1 to \nWeek\n2 D\n85\nCDI Diagnosis (stool \ntest for toxigenic C. \n\nX\nX\nX\nX\nX\nX\nX\n\nPhysical examination\nX\nX\nX\nX\nVital Signs (pre- & post-\ninfusion)\n\nNon-serious AE \n<------------------------------------X --------------------------\nX\n\n<-------------------------------- X ------------------------\n\nX\n\n----------> (All medications)\n\nSerious AE Assessment\n<------------------------------------------------ X ----------------------------------------------\nX\nCollection of Prior / \nConcomitant Medication \nUse\n\n<------------ X -------------->\n(Medications to treat CDI, \nother antibacterials, anti-\ndiarrheal and excluded\n\nX\n\n12-Lead ECG (pre- & \npost-infusion)\n\nSafety Lab Sample\nX\nX\nX\nX\nX\n\nBlood sample for anti-\ndrug antibody (ADA)\nX\nX\nX\nX\n\n80\n\nReference ID: 3989587\n\n\f\nClinical Review\nShrimant Mishra, MD, MPH (Efficacy)\nHiwot, Hiruy, MD, PhD (Safety)\nBLA 761046\nBezlotoxumab\n\nLoose Stool Count\n<--------------------------------Daily loose stool count by the patient with stool count log-----------------\nAE – adverse event, \nSource: Adapted from Table 9-4, study P002 clinical study report\n\n81\n\nReference ID: 3989587\n\n\f\nClinical Review\n\nShrimant Mishra, MD, MPH (Efficacy)\nHiwot, Hiruy, MD, PhD (Safety)\n\nBLA 761046\n\nBezlotoxumab\n\n8.4. Safety Results\n\n8.4.1. Deaths\n\nPhase 1 and 2 Studies\nThere were no deaths reported in the four Phase 1 studies (P020, P004, P005, P006) involving\nadministration of bezlo alone or in combination with acto.\n\nIn study P017, the placebo-controlled Phase 2 trial where subjects received combination of\nacto+bezlo, there were a total of 14 deaths, with 7 deaths (6.9%) in 101 actot+bezlo-treated\nsubjects, and 7 (7.1%) in 99 placebo-treated subjects in the 12-weeks post infusion of study drug.\nTwo of the deaths were due to serious AEs that were deemed possibly drug-related by the\ninvestigator; these subjects are marked with asterisk in the table 38. There was one additional\ndeath in the placebo group about 7 months post infusion and this subject was not included in the\nanalysis.\n\nList of deaths that occurred in the actot+bezlo arm in P017 are summarized in the table below.\n\nTable 38: Summary of Deaths in Actoxumab+Bezlotoxumab arm of Study P017\n\nSubject Age | Cause Study | Study | Relationship to Study Drug\nID (yrs), | of Day | Day of\nsex Death of Death - - -\n(F/M) AE Investigator | Applicant- | Reviewer\nonset -defined defined -defined\n02030401* | 87,F | Urosepsis, 4 15 Possibly Unlikely | Unlikely\nPneumonia related related related\nwith para-\npneumonic\neffusions\n02091001 | 83,M | Respiratory 24 33 Unrelated Unrelated | Unlikely\nfailure related\n02530204 | 74,M | Respiratory 21 35 Unlikely Unlikely | Unlikely\ndistress, VRE related related related\nsepsis, severe\nCOPD\n02620908* | 79,M | Sepsis, kidney | 9 12 Possibly Unlikely | Unlikely\ninfarction, related related related\n\nReference ID: 3989587\n\n82\n\n\f\nClinical Review\nShrimant Mishra, MD, MPH (Efficacy)\nHiwot, Hiruy, MD, PhD (Safety)\nBLA 761046\nBezlotoxumab\n\nembolism, \natherosclerosis\n02620910\n83, M\nSudden death\n45\n45\nUnlikely \nrelated\n\nUnlikely \nrelated\n\n22\n\n24\nUnlikely \nrelated\n\nUnlikely \nrelated\n\nUnlikely \nrelated\n\nUnlikely \nrelated\n02921701\n39, F\nCardio-\npulmonary \narrest\n\n4\n\nP. jirovecii \npneumonia\n\n02921709\n101,\nM\n\n8\n8\nUnlikely \nrelated\n\nUnlikely \nrelated\n\nUnlikely \nrelated\n\nCOPD\nCardiac failure \ncongestive\n\n*denotes the two subjects with serious and drug-related AEs as assessed by the \ninvestigator.\n\nSource:  Sponsor’s “Narratives of Deaths” submitted for study P017 (Section14.3.3.1)\n\nThe death narratives for the two subjects with serious AE resulted in death that were possibly \nrelated to study drug and as assessed by the investigator are presented below: \n\nSubject 203-0401 was an 87-year-old female with significant cardiac disease (s/p \ncoronary bypass grafting, congestive heart failure, atrial fibrillation, s/p pace maker \nplacement, mitral regurgitation), deep venous thrombosis, hypertension, chronic renal \nfailure, and recurrent urinary tract infection. She was hospitalized and diagnosed with C. \ndifficile colitis associated with acute chronic renal failure secondary to volume depletion. \nAfter discharge to a skilled nursing facility, the subject was re-hospitalized on study day \n4 with dyspnea and respiratory distress and found to have a left pleural effusion, \nsignificant leukocytosis, acute renal insufficiency and pyuria. The subject was diagnosed \nwith urosepsis and pneumonia with a parapneumonic effusion. The subject expired on \nstudy day 15 after life support measures were withdrawn.  The event was assessed by the \nInvestigator as possibly related due to the temporal relationship of the event to the study \ninfusion. The Sponsor’s medical reviewer assessed the event as unlikely related to the \nstudy infusion. \n\nSubject 262-0908 was a 79-year-old male with COPD, hypertension, artherosclerosis, s/p \nrecent small bowel obstruction hospitalized for possible ischemic colitis and later \ndiagnosed with C. difficile colitis. During the hospitalization the subject experienced \nworsening leukocytosis and on study day 9 was diagnosed with left renal infarct vs. \npyelonephritis and new onset of dysarthritic speech. The subject was placed in end of \nlife/comfort care and expired on study day 12. The immediate cause of death was sepsis; \nunderlying causes were kidney infarction, embolism and atherosclerosis. The Investigator \nassessed the event as possibly related to study infusion and unexpected. The Sponsor’s \nmedical reviewer assessed the event as unlikely related to the study infusion\n\n83\n\nReference ID: 3989587\n\n\f\nClinical Review\nShrimant Mishra, MD, MPH (Efficacy)\nHiwot, Hiruy, MD, PhD (Safety)\nBLA 761046\nBezlotoxumab\n\nReviewer Comment:  Though relationship to the study drug cannot be ruled out in the above two \ndeaths, both subjects had underlying co-morbidities that could explain the AEs. In all of the 7 \ndeaths, there were co-morbidities that could contribute to and explain the fatal outcome; hence, \nthere were no deaths that appeared related to the study drug. \n\nPhase 3 Studies\nOverall, there were 212 deaths during the Phase 3 trials (Figure 1). Of these, 166 deaths (77 in \nstudy P001 and 89 in study P002) occurred due to AE reported in the 12-weeks post-infusion \nfollow-up period.   The remaining 46 deaths occurred in the following groups:  deaths in subjects \nexcluded by sponsor for not meeting reporting criteria (n=9); death during the extension phase of \nstudy P002 (n=9), deaths among subjects that received acto alone (n=28).  Table 39 and Figure 2 \nsummarize the deaths that occurred in the two Phase 3 trials. \n\nTable 39: Mortality Rate in Each Arm of the Safety Population over the 12-week Study \nPeriod\n\nBezlo\nActo+Bezlo\nPlacebo\nAny Bezlo \nexposure\n\n5/100(5%)\n2/112(1.8%)\n2/83(2.4%)\n3/212(3.3%)\n\nActoa\nN=236\nOver 12 week study period\nP001\n31/390(8%)\n20/387(5.2%)\n26/400(6.5%)\n51/777(6.6%)\n27/235(11.5%)\nP002\n25/396(6.3%)\n31/390(8%)\n33/381(8.7%)\n56/786(7.1%)\n1/1(100%) b\nTotal\n56/786 (7.1%)\n51/777(6.6%)\n59/781(7.5%)\n107/1563(6.8%)\n28/236 (11.9%)\nDuring Extension phase\nStudy \nP002\n\na The acto arm is not included in the safety population and data for acto arm is presented for \ncomparison.\nb This subject in study P002 was inadvertently given actoxumab only\n\nThe extension phase was comprised of 295 subjects with 112 subjects in the acto+bezlo arm, 100 \nsubjects in the bezlo arm and 83 subjects in the placebo group; in general, subjects in the \nextension cohort were younger and had fewer bad prognostic factors. The mortality rate by arm \nfor this group was as follows: 2 (1.8%) in the acto+bezlo arm, 5 in the bezlo arm (5%), and 2 in \nthe placebo arm (2.4%). \n\n84\n\nReference ID: 3989587\n\n\f\nClinical Review\nShrimant Mishra, MD, MPH (Efficacy)\nHiwot, Hiruy, MD, PhD (Safety)\nBLA 761046\nBezlotoxumab\n\nFigure 2: Overall Deaths during Phase 3 Trials\n\n85\n\nReference ID: 3989587\n\n\f\nClinical Review\nShrimant Mishra, MD, MPH (Efficacy)\nHiwot, Hiruy, MD, PhD (Safety)\nBLA 761046\nBezlotoxumab\n\nTable 40, 41, and 42 describe baseline demographic, clinical and laboratory features of subjects \nwho died during the 12-week follow-up period. It should be noted that data for the acto only arm \nis provided for comparison and is not part of the safety population.\n\nTab