nfection\n15 (1.9)\n9 (1.2)\n\n94\n\nReference ID: 3989587\n\n\f\nClinical Review\nShrimant Mishra, MD, MPH (Efficacy)\nHiwot, Hiruy, MD, PhD (Safety)\nBLA 761046\nBezlotoxumab\n\nMedDRA Preferred Term\n\nBezlo\nN=786\nn (%)\n\nPlacebo\nN=781\nn (%)\nDiarrhea\n16 (2)\n12 (1.53)\nAbdominal pain\n7 (0.89)\n4 (0.51)\nAnemia\n5 (0.64)\n3 (0.38)\nChronic kidney disease\n5 (0.64)\n5 (0.64)\nRespiratory failure\n5 (0.64)\n6 (0.77)\nAcute kidney injury\n6 (0.76)\n10 (1.28)\nPneumonia and pneumonia aspiration \n17 (2.2)\n22(2.8)\nSepsis and urosepsis\n14(1.78)\n27(3.46)\nClostridium difficile infection\n24 (3.1)\n54 (6.9)\n*Cardiac failure terms “cardiac failure”, “cardiac failure acute”, “cardiac failure congestive” and \n“cardiac failure chronic” were combined.\nMedDRA=Medical Dictionary for Regulatory Activities\n\nTable 47: Serious Adverse Events that Occurred in the Actoxumab+Bezlotoxumab Arm\n\nMedDRA Preferred Term\n\nActo+Bezlo\nN=777\nn (%)\n\nPlacebo\nN=781\nn (%)\nCardiac failure*\n17 (2.19)\n7 (0.9)\nBacteremia\n6 (0.77)\n3 (0.38)\nUrinary tract infections\n11 (1.42)\n9 (1.15) \nAbdominal pain\n6 (0.77)\n4 (0.51)\nChronic obstructive pulmonary disease\n5 (0.64)\n4(0.51)\nRespiratory failure\n5 (0.64)\n6 (0.77)\nDiarrhea\n10 (1.28)\n 12 (1.54)\nSeptic shock\n7 (0.9)\n10 (1.28)\nPneumonia and pneumonia aspiration\n17(2.19)\n22 (2.81)\nSepsis and urosepsis\n11 (1.42)\n27 (3.46)\nClostridium difficile infection\n31 (4)\n54 (6.9)\n*Cardiac failure terms “cardiac failure”, “cardiac failure acute”, “cardiac failure congestive” and \n“cardiac failure chronic” were combined.\nMedDRA=Medical Dictionary for Regulatory Activities\n\n95\n\nReference ID: 3989587\n\n\f\nClinical Review\nShrimant Mishra, MD, MPH (Efficacy)\nHiwot, Hiruy, MD, PhD (Safety)\nBLA 761046\nBezlotoxumab\n\nTable 48: Serious Adverse Events that Occurred in >2 Subjects in the Actoxumab Arm\n\nMedDRA Preferred Term\n\nActo\nN=236\nn (%)\n\nPlacebo\nN=781\nn (%)\nClostridium difficle infection\n26 (11.02)\n54 (6.91)\nCardiac failure*\n7 (2.97)\n7 (0.9)\nUrinary tract infection\n6 (2.54)\n9 (1.15)\nCerebrovascular accident\n3 (1.27)\n0\nConstipation\n3 (1.27)\n0\nAbdominal pain\n4 (1.69)\n4 (0.51)\nNausea\n3 (1.27)\n2 (0.26)\nDiarrhea\n6 (2.54)\n12 (1.54)\nAcute kidney injury\n4 (1.69)\n10 (1.28)\nSeptic shock\n3 (1.27)\n10 (1.28)\nPneumonia and pneumonia aspiration\n8\n22\nSepsis and urosepsis\n10\n27\n*Cardiac failure terms “cardiac failure”, “cardiac failure acute”, “cardiac failure congestive” and \n“cardiac failure chronic” were combined.\nMedDRA=Medical Dictionary for Regulatory Activities\n\nReviewer Comment: Overall, there is no major imbalance in the frequency of serious adverse \nevents among the three arms:  29.4%, 27.3% o and 32.7% of subjects in the bezlo, acto+bezlo \nand placebo arm, respectively, experienced at least one SAE in the 12-week study period. In all \nthree arms.The infection and infestation and gastrointestinal MedDRA System Organ Classes \naccounted for the majority of the SAEs. A detailed look of the SAEs revealed cardiac failure, \nurinary tract infection, diarrhea, abdominal pain  to have occurred more frequently in the bezlo \narm as compared to the placebo arm. From these, cardiac failure showed the largest risk \ndifference, with 17 subjects (2.2%) in the bezlo arm as compared to placebo (n=7, 0.009%). \nSame trend of more subjects with cardiac failure was seen in the acto+bezlo arm (n=17, 2.2%) \nas compared to placebo.  Of note, preferred terms that describe cardiac failure (cardiac failure, \ncardiac failure acute, cardiac failure congestive, cardiac failure chronic) were combined for the \nanalysis. Please refer to section 8.5.3 for detailed subgroup analysis of subjects by presence or \nabsence of congestive heart failure (CHF) at baseline.\n\nNarratives of SAEs were reviewed, including the four acto+bezlo subjects who had SAEs \ndeemed related to the study drug by the investigator.   Synopses of these four subjects are \npresented below:\n\n96\n\nReference ID: 3989587\n\n\f\nClinical Review\n\nShrimant Mishra, MD, MPH (Efficacy)\nHiwot, Hiruy, MD, PhD (Safety)\n\nBLA 761046\n\nBezlotoxumab\n\ne 91-year-old Asian woman with multiple co-morbidities including hypertension, and\ndyslipidemia received acto+bezlo infusion on day 3 of standard of care therapy with\nmetronidazole. On day 8, she displayed signs and symptoms of cerebral infarction. She\nconcurrently had atrial fibrillation. She was treated for cerebral infarction and recovered\nwith sequelae. The atrial fibrillation was reported ongoing and was assessed by the\ninvestigator as being moderate and related to the study therapy.\n\ne 73 year-old white woman with cutaneous lupus erythematous, hypercholesterolemia,\nsevere hypoproteinemia received actot+bezlo on day 7 of SoC therapy with\nmetronidazole. She subsequently developed colitis and anasarca due to\nhypoalbuminemeia on day 21. She was also diagnosed with alveolar hemorrhage on day\n22. The alveolar hemorrhage resolved and she was discharged on day 35. The alveolar\nhemorrhage was assessed by the investigator as moderate and related to the study\ntherapy.\n\ne 84-year-old Asian man with stomach cancer and adhesive ileus was diagnosed with\nCDI while hospitalized. He received acto+bezlo infusion 5 days after initiation SoC\nwith PO metronidazole. He was discharged on Day 3. On day 34, the subject\nexperienced 2 loose stools (diarrhea) which tested positive for CDI. He was\nhospitalized again on day 34 and treated with PO metronidazole. The subject\nrecovered and was discharged on day 36. The investigator stated that the\nrelationship of the CDI recurrence to study medication was unknown due to the\ntemporal sequence of AE to administration of study medication.\n\ne 85 year old white man with prostate adenocarcinoma, asthma, atrial fibrillation and\nhepatitis received acto+bezlo infusion 3 days after initiation of standard of care PO\nmetronidazole treatment. While in the extension phase of the study, on day 249, he\nfell at home, and was hospitalized for fractured right femur. He underwent\nhemiarthroplasty and was discharged from the hospital on Day 258. The\ninvestigator noted the event (femur fracture) could be related to study medication.\n\nReviewer Comment: Although the relationship between the SAEs and the study drugs cannot be\ncompletely ruled out, all four subjects had other concurrent illnesses or demographics that could\nhave contributed to the SAEs. No clear relationship between the SAEs and the study drug could\nbe concluded.\n\n8.4.3. Dropouts and/or Discontinuations Due to Adverse Effects\n\nBezlo, acto+bezlo and placebo (0.9% sodium chloride) were given as one-time infusion in their\nrespective arms; hence, drug discontinuation in the traditional term is not applicable. The\ninfusion volume ranged from 200-250ml, and an in-line filter was used for the infusions. There\nwere 47 infusion interruptions, with 9, 34 and 4 interruptions in the bezlo, acto+bezlo and acto\narm, respectively. Majority of these were due to technical difficulties. Sponsor’s explanation for\nthe higher technical difficulties in the acto+bezlo arm was propensity of acto to form visible\nparticles at low concentrations. This is supported by the higher treatment interruptions seen in\n\nReference ID: 3989587\n\n97\n\n\f\nClinical Review\nShrimant Mishra, MD, MPH (Efficacy)\nHiwot, Hiruy, MD, PhD (Safety)\nBLA 761046\nBezlotoxumab\n\nacto only arm in study P001 (27/236) and formation of visible particles at low concentration. \n\nThere was only one infusion interruption that led to permanent discontinuation. This occurred in \na 32 year-old- white male with AIDS who developed ventricular arrhythmia, chills, and dizziness \nwith an onset approximately 36 minutes after the start of bezlo infusion.  His pre-infusion \nelectrocardiogram (ECG) showed atrial and ventricular rate of 103 beats per minute (bpm). The \nbezlo infusion was discontinued, and the subject was treated with fenistil (an \nantihistamine/anticholinergic drug), prednisolone, and ranitidine intravenously. The chills s and \nventricular tachyarrhythmia resolved within 2-5minutes, and dizziness resolved within 90 \nminutes.  ECG performed about 30 minutes after discontinuing the infusion showed atrial and \nventricular rates of 99, and signs of pre-excitation syndrome.  The investigator and the sponsor \nclassified the event as drug-related. \n\nReviewer Comment: There is a strong temporal relationship between the infusion of bezlo and \noccurrence of the serious adverse events of ventricular arrhythmia, chills, and dizziness in the \nsubject described above. The subject experienced significant infusion reaction and the reviewer \nagrees with the investigator and sponsor’s classification of the event as drug-related. \n\n Table 49 summarizes the overall subject disposition in the safety population.\n\nTable 49: Subject Disposition in the Safety Population\n\nBezlo\nN=786\nn, (%)\n\nActo+Bezlo\nN=777\nn, (%)\n\nPlacebo\nN=781\nn, (%)\n\nTotal\nN=2344\nn, (%)\n\n64 (8.1)\n54 (6.9)\n62 (7.9)\n180 (7.7)\n28 (11.9)\n\nActo\nN=236\nn, (%)\nCompleted\n675 (85.9)\n664(85.5)\n654 (83.7)\n1993 (85)\n201 (85.2)\nDeath a\n52 (6.6)\n47 (6)\n57 (7.3)\n156 (6.7)\n27 (11.4)\nDeath including \nsubjects reported in \nother categories b\n\n34 (4.3)\n30 (3.9)\n39 (5)\n103 (4.4)\n3 (1.3)\n\nWithdrawal by \nsubject\n\nLost to follow-up\n21 (2.7)\n26 (3.3)\n22 (2.8)\n69 (2.9)\n2 (0.85)\nPhysician decision\n3 (0.38)\n5 (0.64)\n3 (0.38)\n11 (0.47)\n1 (0.42)\nAdverse event\n1 (0.13)\n1 (0.13)\n2 (0.26)\n4 (0.17)\n1 (0.42)\nProtocol violation\n0\n3 (0.39)\n2 (0.26)\n5 (0.21)\n0\nProgressive disease\n0\n1 (0.13)\n2 (0.26)\n3 (0.13)\n0\nLack of efficacy\n0\n0\n0\n0\n1 (0.42)\n\n98\n\nReference ID: 3989587\n\n\f\nClinical Review\n\nShrimant Mishra, MD, MPH (Efficacy)\nHiwot, Hiruy, MD, PhD (Safety)\n\nBLA 761046\n\nBezlotoxumab\n\na As reported by the sponsor\n\n> Includes all deaths among subjects who received study treatment including those who\ndied outside of a 90-day follow-up period, died during extension Phase of study P002,\nwithdrew consent, or were reported under the completed or discontinued due to AE\ncategories. The numbers in this row should not be included when counting the total.\n\nOf note, in addition to the subjects classified in the death category in Table 49 , there were\nsubjects who died but reported under other disposition categories. Thus, in the bezlo arm, four\nadditional subjects had fatal outcomes (n=2 from the completed category, n=1 from discontinued\ndue to AE category, and n=1 from withdrawal by subject category).\n\nAmong the three subjects who were removed from the study by physician decision, 2 were\nreportedly non-compliant with study visits/ procedures; there was no reported reason for the third\nsubject.\n\nReviewer Comment: Overall, about 85% of subjects in each arm completed the study. There was\nno significant imbalance in subject withdrawn from study in the active arms, including the bezlo\narm, when compared to placebo.\n\n8.4.4. Significant Adverse Events\n\nPlease refer to the laboratory section for analysis of hematological AEs by study arm. In general,\nthere did not appear to be an imbalance in degree of anemia, thrombocytopenia, or neutropenia\ncomparing the bezlo arm to the placebo arm.\n\n8.4.5. Treatment Emergent Adverse Events and Adverse Reactions\n\nAmong the 2344 subjects that make up the safety population, 1622 reported one or more\ntreatment emergent adverse events over the 12-weekstudy period, with 5394 events reported. Of\nthese, 1418 subjects reported TEAE during the first 4 weeks of the study, with total of 3819\nevents (1209 in the actot+bezlo, 1319 in bezlo and 1291 in placebo arms).\n\nTable 50 summarizes the number of AE events and subjects that experienced at least one AE\nover the 4-week period.\n\nTable 50: Treatment Emergent Adverse Events (TEAE) Over the 4-week and 12-week\nStudy Period\n\n4-week period 12-week period\nNumber of reported AEs 3819 5349\n\n99\n\nReference ID: 3989587\n\n\f\nClinical Review\nShrimant Mishra, MD, MPH (Efficacy)\nHiwot, Hiruy, MD, PhD (Safety)\nBLA 761046\nBezlotoxumab\n\n1418\n1622\n\nActo*\n159\n716\nActo+Bezlo\n1209\n1696\nBezlo\n1319\n1803\nPlacebo\n1291\n1850\nNumber of individuals with \nAE\n\nActo*\n483\n184\nActo+Bezlo\n455\n529\nBezlo\n485\n548\nPlacebo\n478\n545\n*Acto arm is not included in the safety population\n\nReviewer Comment:  It should be noted that the sponsor collected all TEAEs in the first 4-week \nof the study; after that, only serious adverse events were reported. Hence, the data for the 12-\nweek period includes all the TEAEs reported in the 4-week period. In general, there does not \nseem to be a significant imbalance in the frequency of adverse events for the bezlo and \nacto+bezlo arm as compared to the placebo arm. \n\nTable 51 summarizes the reported TEAEs by MedDRA SOC classification.\n\nTable 51: Treatment Emergent Adverse Events by System Organ Class\n\nSOC\nBezlo\nN=786\nn, (%)\n\nActo+Bezlo\nN=777\nn, (%)\n\nPlacebo\nN=781\nn, (%)\n\n178(22.6)\n164(21.1)\n149(19.1)\n69(29.2)\n\nActo\nN=236\nn, %)\nGastrointestinal\n360(45.8)\n332(42.7)\n319(40.8)\n150(63.6)\nInfection & infestation\n304(38.7)\n268(34.5)\n340(43.5)\n120(50.8)\nGeneral disorders & administration site \nconditions\n\n104(13.2)\n85(10.9)\n99(12.7)\n38(16.1)\n\nNervous system disorders\n104(13.2)\n123(15.8)\n112(14.3)\n46(19.5)\nRespiratory, thoracic & mediastinal \ndisorders\n\nInvestigations\n96(12.2)\n80(10.3)\n86(11)\n27(11.4)\nSkin & subcutaneous tissue disorders\n68(8.65)\n56(7.210\n88(11.3)\n24(10.2)\n\nMetabolism & nutrition disorder\n66(8.4)\n80*10.3)\n93(11.9)\n34(14.4)\n\n64(8.14)\n78(10)\n72(9.22)\n23(9.75)\n\nMusculoskeletal and connective tissue \ndisorders\n\nCardiac disorders\n54(6.87)\n55(7.08)\n56(7.17)\n21(8.9)\nRenal and urinary disorders\n54(6.87)\n40(5.15)\n59(7.55)\n12(5.08)\n\n100\n\nRefe