-times the upper limit of \nnormal, and ≥50% increase in alkaline phosphates  for each arm by study visit are summarized in \nTable 55. \n\nTable 55: Liver investigations by Study Visit\n\nBezlo\n\nPlacebo\n\nActo\n\nn (%)\n\nActo+\nBezlo\nn (%)\n\nn (%)\n\nn (%)\nDay 4 visit\nN=756\nN=740\nN=749\nN=226\nALT 3X ULN\n10(1.3)\n12 (1.6)\n18 (2.4)\n3 (1.3)\nALT 5X ULN\n2 (0.26)\n4 (0.54)\n4 (0.53)\n1 (0.4)\nALT 10X ULN\n0\n0\n2 (0.27)\n0\nAST 3X ULN\n10 (1.3)\n10 (1.4)\n13 (1.7)\n0\nAST 5X ULN\n3 (0.4)\n4 (0.54)\n4 (0.53)\n0\nAST 10X ULN\n1 (0.13)\n2 (0.27)\n2 (0.27)\n0\nTotal bilirubin 2X ULN\n7 (0.93)\n9 (1.2)\n8 (1.1)\n4(1.8)\nAlkaline phosphatase  2XULN\n29 (3.8)\n23 (3.1)\n24 (3.2)\n7 (3.1)\nDay 11 visit\nN=733\nN=713\nN=728\nN=219\nALT 3X ULN\n7 (0.95)\n11 (1.5)\n9 (1.23)\n1 (0.46)\nALT 5X ULN\n0\n4 (0.56)\n6 (0.82)\n1 (0.46)\nALT 10X ULN\n0\n0\n5 (0.69)\n0\nAST 3X ULN\n5 (0.68)\n9 (1.26)\n9 (1.2)\n2 (0.91)\nAST 5X ULN\n2 (0.27)\n2 (0.28)\n6 (0.82)\n1 (0.46)\nAST 10X ULN\n1 (0.14)\n0\n3 (0.41)\n0\nTotal bilirubin 2X ULN\n5 (0.68)\n13 (1.8)\n6 (0.82)\n6 (2.7)\nAlkaline phosphatase 2XULN\n25 (3.4)\n26 (3.6)\n23 (3.2)\n6 (2.7)\nWeek 4\nN=677\nN=671\nN=666\nN=210\nALT 3X ULN\n6 (0.89)\n10(1.5)\n4(0.6)\n3 (1.4)\nALT 5X ULN\n0\n4(0.6)\n2(0.3)\n1(0.48)\nALT 10X ULN\n0\n1(0.15)\n0\n0\nAST 3X ULN\n4(0.59)\n9(1.3)\n3(0.45)\n2(0.95)\nAST 5X ULN\n1(0.15)\n5(0.75)\n0\n0\nAST 10X ULN\n0\n1(0.15)\n0\n0\nTotal bilirubin 2X ULN\n9(1.3)\n101.5)\n0\n4(1.9)\nAlkaline phosphatase >2XULN\n24(3.5)\n26(3.9)\n24(3.6)\n8(3.8)\n\n103\n\nReference ID: 3989587\n\n\f\nClinical Review\nShrimant Mishra, MD, MPH (Efficacy)\nHiwot, Hiruy, MD, PhD (Safety)\nBLA 761046\nBezlotoxumab\n\nALT: Alanine transaminase; AST: Aspartate transaminase; N indicates number of subjects with \nthe available lab for the visit in the specified arm; n indicates the number of subjects with the lab \nabnormality in that arm. \n\nReviewer Comment: Overall, there is no significant difference in the liver labs. There were \nslightly more subjects in the placebo arm with elevated transaminases and total bilirubin \ncompared to the bezlo or acto+bezlo arm for each study visit. Since bezlo is a monoclonal \nantibody, it is catabolized in the body, and not metabolized in the liver. Hence, we did not \nanticipate significant impact on the liver, and the results support the hypothesis.\n\nAdditional analyses were performed to assess for potential drug-induced liver injury (DILI), and \nliver abnormalities that fulfill the criteria for Hy’s law are presented in Table 56.\n\nTable 56: Subjects that Fulfilled Hy's Law by study Visit and study arm\n\nPlacebo \nActo\n\nVisit \nBezlo\nActo+\nBezlo\n\nSubjects with ALT or AST 3X \nULN AND total bilirubin \n2XULN \n\nDay 4\n1\n2\n3\n0\nDay 11\n0\n2\n1\n1\n\nWeek 4\n1\n3\n0\n1\n\nPlacebo \nActo\n\nVisit \nBezlo\nActo+\nBezlo\n\nSubjects with ALT or AST 3X \nULN AND total bilirubin \n2XULN AND ALP within the \nnormal range\n\nDay 4\n0\n0\n1\n0\nDay 11\n0\n0\n1\n0\n\nWeek 4\n0\n2\n0\n0\n\nULN=Upper limit of normal; ALP=alkaline phosphatase; ALT=alanine transaminase; \nAST=Aspartate transaminase\n\nFor day 4 visit, one subject from the bezlo arm, a 78 year-old female with multiple co-\nmorbidities including dyslipidemia and congestive heart failure,  had a 3 times the upper limit of \nnormal value for  aminotransferases and 2 times the normal value for bilirubin. This subject also \nhad four-fold increase in alkaline phosphatase from baseline (>3xULN). The subject’s ALT \nnormalized during day 11 visit, with continued elevation in ALP and bilirubin; the subject \nwithdrew from the study between week 3 and 4. \n\n  Reviewer Comment: In addition to elevated transaminases, the subject also had elevated \nalkaline phosphatase; it is possible that the subject had cholestasis and this may have \ncontributed to elevated transaminases. \n\n104\n\nReference ID: 3989587\n\n\f\nClinical Review\nShrimant Mishra, MD, MPH (Efficacy)\nHiwot, Hiruy, MD, PhD (Safety)\nBLA 761046\nBezlotoxumab\n\nFor  week 4 visit,  one subject from the bezlo arm, a 32 year-old female with a history of \ncirrhosis, thalassemia and iron overload,  had a 4 times the ULN  value for ALT(130 IU/L) and a \n3 times the ULN for AST(92 IU/L), with bilirubin 2 times the ULN(2.3mg/DL) and slightly \nelevated ALP of 126 IU/L(ULN defined as 105IU/L).  The hepatic enzyme elevations were \nattributed to the subject’s underlying conditions.  The subject was reported to have recovered.\n\nReviewer Comment:  The reviewer agrees with the sponsor’s assessment of subject’s underlying \nconditions (cirrhosis) contributing to the observed hepatic enzyme elevations. \n\nAssessment of Kidney Function\n\nChanges from baseline in kidney function were evaluated for each study visit. The results are \nsummarized in Table 57 and Table 58. \n\nTable 57: Clinically Significant Changes in Creatinine from Baseline\n\nBezlo\nProportion, (%)\n\nActo+Bezlo\nProportion, ( %)\n\nPlacebo\nProportion, (%)\n\nActo\nProportion, \n(%)\nIncrease by≥0.3mg/dL\n\nDay 4\n59/760(7.8)\n55/741(7.4)\n49/750(6.5)\n23/226 (10.2)\nDay11\n91/739(12.3)\n67/718(9.3)\n78/731(10.7)\n38/220 (17.3)\nWeek 4\n81/680(11.9)\n83/676(12.3)\n84/671(12.5)\n42/210 (20)\nPost baseline creatinine>2mg/dL\n\nDay 4\n70/760(9.2)\n41/741(5.5)\n57/750(7.6)\n21/226 (9.3)\nDay 11\n68/739(9.2)\n42/718(5.8)\n65/731(8.9)\n18/220 (8.2)\nWeek 4\n61/680(9)\n40/676(5.9)\n50/671(7.5)\n19/210 (9)\n\nTable 58: Changes in Blood Urea Nitrogen (BUN) from Baseline\n\nBezlo\nProportion, (%)\n\nActo+Bezlo\nProportion, (%)\n\nPlacebo\nProportion, (%)\n\nActo\nProportion, (%)\n\nBUN increase by≥50%\n\nDay 4\n35/760 (4.6)\n24/741 (3.2) \n32/750 (4.3)\n13/226 (5.8)\nDay11\n74/739 (10)\n63/718 (8.8)\n65/731 (8.9)\n35/220 (15.9)\nWeek 4\n87/680 (12.8)\n80/676 (11.8)\n88/671 (13.1)\n37/210 (17.6)\n\n105\n\nReference ID: 3989587\n\n\f\nClinical Review\nShrimant Mishra, MD, MPH (Efficacy)\nHiwot, Hiruy, MD, PhD (Safety)\nBLA 761046\nBezlotoxumab\n\nPost baseline BUN >31mg/dL\n\nDay 4\n92/760 (12.1)\n55/741 (7.4)\n83/750 (11.1)\n26/226 (11.5)\nDay 11\n85/739 (11.5)\n62/718 (8.6)\n78/731 (10.7)\n32/220 (14.4)\nWeek 4\n81/680 (11.9)\n66/676 (9.8)\n91/671 (13.6)\n26/210 (12.4)\n\nReviewer Comment: There is no significant difference in creatinine and BUN changes from \nbaseline when comparing the bezlo arm to the placebo at each study visit. The acto+bezlo arm \nhad overall slightly less subjects with increase in creatinine and BUN from baseline. \n\nAssessment of Hematologic Labs\n \nTable 59 summarizes significant changes in hemoglobin, platelet count, and neutrophil counts \nfrom baseline for subjects in each study arm.\n\nTable 59: Changes in Hemoglobin, Platelet, and Neutrophil Count from Baseline\n\nBezlo\n\nPlacebo\n\nActo\n\nn (%)\n\nActo+\nBezlo\nn (%)\n\nn (%)\n\nn (%)\nHemoglobin decrease by ≥3gm/dL from baseline\n\nDay 4\n2/738 (0.3)\n3/722 (0.4)\n5/737 (0.7)\n1/217(0.5)\nDay11\n9/718 (1.3)\n10/690(1.4)\n10/710 (1.4)\n4/213(1.9)\nWeek 4\n7/661 (1.1)\n8/660 (1.2)\n13/648(2)\n6/207(2.9)\nPost baseline platelet count <100x103/µL\n\nDay 4\n31/716 (4.3)\n30/694 (4.3)\n36/720 (5)\n6/210(2.9)\nDay 11\n35/703 (5)\n25/671 (3.7)\n28/695 (4)\n8/203 (3.9)\nWeek 4\n26/641 (4.1)\n21/650 (3.2)\n27/638 (4.2)\n9/200 (4.5)\nPost baseline neutrophil count <1000/µL\n\nDay 4\n12/728 (1.6)\n10/717 (1.4)\n7/722 (0.9)\n2/216 (0.9)\nDay 11\n11/708 (1.6)\n14/685 (2)\n14/702 (2)\n3/210 (1.4)\nWeek 4\n5/649 (0.8)\n9/656 (1.4)\n11/642(1.7)\n4/203 (2)\n\nReviewer Comment: There was no imbalance in the number of subjects with drop in hemoglobin, \nthrombocytopenia or neutropenia when comparing the bezlo arm to placebo. \n\n106\n\nReference ID: 3989587\n\n\f\nClinical Review\n\nShrimant Mishra, MD, MPH (Efficacy)\nHiwot, Hiruy, MD, PhD (Safety)\n\nBLA 761046\n\nBezlotoxumab\n\n8.4.7. Vital Signs\n\nVital signs were recorded at baseline, 30-minutes after start of infusion, end of infusion,\nand at day 4 and day11 visits. A summary of clinically significant changes from baseline in\n\nvital signs are presented in Table 60 and\n\nTable 61 for 30-minute post infusion and end of infusion, respectively.\n\nTable 60: Changes from Baseline in Vital Signs 30-minutes after Start of Infusion\n\nBezlo Actot+ Placebo Acto\nBezlo\nN=786, N=777 N=781 N=236\nn(%) n (%) n(%) n(%)\nDrop in SBP by 1 (0.13) 5 (0.64) 1 (0.13) 0\n>30% from baseline\nSBP<90mmHg 9 (1.1) 11 (1.4) 7 (0.9) 3 (1.3)\nDrop from BL>SmmHg | 3 (0.38) 5 (0.64) 3 (0.38) 1 (0.4)\nDrop in DBP by >20mmHg 17 (2.2) 19 (2.4) 24 (3.1) 3 (1.3)\nDBP<60mmHg 89 (11.3) 95 (12.2) 95 (12.2) 31 (13.1)\nDrop from BL by >5mmHg | 40 (5.1) 49 (6.3) 48 (6.1) 8 (3.4)\nHR>120bpm 3 (0.38) 2 (0.25) 6 (0.77) 3 (1.3)\nIncrease from BL by =10bpm | 2 (0.25) 1 (0.13) 2 (0.25) 1 (0.42)\nHR<60bpm 68 (8.7) 74 (9.5) 64 (8.2) 20 (8.5)\nDrop from BL by >10bpm | 6 (0.76) 12 (1.5) 12 (1.5) 7 (3)\nTemp >37.8°C 25 (3.2) 15 (1.9) 16 (2) 3 (1.3)\nIncrease from BL by =1°C | 3 (0.38) 3 (0.39) 0 1 (0.42)\nTemp<35 °C 2 (0.25) 0 3 (0.38) 0\nDrop from BL by=1°C | 0 0 2 (0.26) 0\nTemp increase by >2°C from BL 1 (0.13) 1(0.13) 0 0\nTemp decrease by >2°C from BL 0 1 (0.13) 1 (0.13) 0\nRR>24 per minute 29 (3.7) 22 (2.8) 25 (3.2) 8 (3.4)\nIncrease from BL by >3 per minute | 14 (1.8) 12 (1.5) 10 (1.3) 4 (1.7)\nRR<12 per minute 12 (1.5) 7 (0.9) 14 (1.8) 0\nDecrease from BL by =3 per minute | 1 (0.13) 2 (0.26) 1 (0.13) 0\n\nBL — baseline\n\nReference ID: 3989587\n\n107\n\n\f\nClinical Review\nShrimant Mishra, MD, MPH (Efficacy)\nHiwot, Hiruy, MD, PhD (Safety)\nBLA 761046\nBezlotoxumab\n\nTable 61: Changes from Baseline in Vital Signs at End of Infusion\n\nBezlo\n\nPlacebo\n\nActo\n\nN=786, \nn (%)\n\nActo+\nBezlo\nN=777\nn (%)\n\nN=781\nn (%)\n\n2 (0.25)\n5 (0.64)\n2 (0.26)\n0 \n\nN=236\nn (%)\nDrop in SBP by \n≥30% from baseline\n\nSBP<90mmHg\n11 (1.4)\n9 (1.16)\n5 (0.64)\n4 (1.7)\nDrop from BL by≥ 5mmHg\n3 (0.38)\n5 (0.6)\n2 (0.26)\n1 (0.42)\nDrop in DBP by ≥20mmHg\n19 (2.4)\n21 (2.7)\n15 (1.9)\n11 (4.7)\nDBP<60mmHg\n86 (10.9)\n83 (10.7)\n82 (10.5)\n34 (14.4)\n\nDrop from BL≥5mmHg 43 (5.5)\n34 (4.34)\n41 (5.2)\n15 (6.4)\n\nHR>120\n4 (0.5)\n4 (0.51)\n5 (0.64)\n2 (0.84)\nIncrease from BL by ≥10bpm\n4 (0.5)\n2 (0.26)\n2 (0.26)\n1 (0.42)\nHR<60\n68 (8.7)\n65 (8.4)\n68 (8.7)\n16 (6.8)\nDrop from BL by ≥10bpm 9 (1.1)\n6 (0.77)\n14 (1.8)\n3 (1.3)\nTemp ≥37.8 oC\n21 (2.7)\n18 (2.3)\n19 (2.4)\n4 (1.7)\nIncrease from BL by  ≥1oC\n8 (1)\n3 (0.39)\n1 (0.13)\n2 (0.84)\nTemp<35 oC\n1 (0.13)\n1 (0.13)\n3 (0.38)\n0\nDrop from BL by  ≥1oC\n0\n0\n1 (0.13)\n0\nIncrease by≥2oC from BL\n2 (0.25)\n0\n0\n0\nDecrease by≥2 oC from BL\n0\n1\n1 (0.13)\n0\nRR>24\n25 (3.2)\n17 (2.2)\n27 (3.5)\n6 (2.5)\nIncrease from BL≥3 per minute 9 (1.1)\n7 (0.9)\n11 (1.4)\n2 (0.84)\nRR<12\n8 (1)\n7 (0.9)\n10 1.3)\n2 (0.84)\nDecrease from BL≥3 per minute 0\n2 (0.26)\n2 (0.26)\n1 (0.42)\nBL - baseline\n\nLooking  at the bezlo arm at 30 minutes after start of infusion, there were 10 subjects that \nfulfilled Sampson’s criteria of decrease in systolic BP with either drop in SBP by 30 % (n=1) or \nSBP< 90mmHg (n=9).  Among those with SBP<90mmHg, 3 had drop from baseline by \n>5mmHg. At the end-of-infusion, there were 13 subjects that met the criteria of a drop by 30% in \nSBP (n=2) or SBP<90mmHg (n=11).  Among those with SBP<90mmHg, three had drop from \nbaseline by >5mmHg\n\n108\n\nReference ID: 3989587\n\n\f\nClinical Review\n\nShrimant Mishra, MD, MPH (Efficacy)\nHiwot, Hiruy, MD, PhD (Safety)\n\nBLA 761046\n\nBezlotoxumab\n\nOne subject in the bezlo arm and 4 subjects in the actot+bezlo arm had drop in SBP by 30% at\nboth 30-minute after start of infusion and end of infusion. Among subjects with SBP less than\n90mmHg, 16 (n=5 in the bezlo arm, 6 in acto+bezlo arm, 3 in placebo arm and 2 in acto arm)\nhad SBP<90mmHg at both 30-minutes after start of infusion and end of infusion. There was\nonly one subject in the bezlo arm that had a drop of>SmmHg from baseline at both times, and\nsynopsis of the patient is presented below.\n\nThe subject in the bezlo arm is a 91 year-old female with multiple comorbidities including\nhypertension, atrial fibrillation, and hypercholesterolemia with the following baseline (pre-\ninfusion) vital signs: BP 150/70 mmHg, HR 88bpm, RR 16 and temperature of 37.2. At 30\nminutes after start of infusion, her blood pressure decreased to 104/60, and heart rate and\nrespiratory rate increased to 160 and 23, respectively. She remained afebrile. At the end of\ninfusion, she had further drop in blood pressure to 90/64 mmHg, heart rate 166 bpm, RR 20 and\ntemperature 37 C. Her concomitant medications on day of infusion included metronidazole,\nfurosemide, terbutaline sulfate, ipratropium bromide, ceftriaxone, and spironolactone.\n\nReviewer Comment: Overall, there was no significant difference between the treatment arms and\nplacebo in regards to changes from baseline of heart rate, systolic blood pressure, diastolic\nblood pressure, body temperature, and respiratory rate. Majority of subjects with SBP below\n90mmHg had baseline SBP below 90mmHg, which likely is due acute illness (infection) rather\nthan infusion reaction. The one bezlo subject presented in the synopsis above appears to have\nhad an infusion reaction with significant changes in SBP and tachycardia; however, there may\nalso be contribution of her vasoactive concomitant medication to the changes in vital signs. In\naddition, the one bezlo subject described in section 8.4.3 that developed ventricular tachycardia\ndizziness and chills during bezlo infusion is considered to have had infusion reaction.\n\n8.4.8. Electrocardiograms (ECGs)\n\nThere was no dedicated QT study during the drug development program. In the Phase 1 studies\nP004, P00S and P006, healthy subjects had ECG monitoring and time-matched pharmacokinetic\nsampling after administration of 10mg/kg to 20mg/kg of acto+bezlo. The sponsor use a QT\ninterval adjusted by population-derived correction (QTcP), and noted a trend of decreasing\nchange from baseline QTc with increasing bezlo serum concentration that was statistically\nsignificant but was felt to not be clinically meaningful.\n\nIn the two Phase 3 studies, electrocardiogram (ECG) measurements were performed pre-infusion\nand within 120 minutes post infusion during the Phase 3 studies.\n\n8.4.9. QT\n\n109\n\nReference ID: 3989587\n\n\f\nClinical Review\n\nShrimant Mishra, MD, MPH (Efficacy)\nHiwot, Hiruy, MD, PhD (Safety)\n\nBLA 761046\nBezlotoxumab\n\nECG was performed just prior to start of infusion (pre-infusion) and within 2 hours after the end\nof infusion; summary of the QT findings corrected for heart rate using Frederica correction is\n\npresented below in Table 62.\n\nTable 62: Summary of QTc Measurements\n\nBezlo Acto+ Placebo Acto\nBezlo\nn,(%) n,(%) n,(%) n, (%)\nBaseline ECG N=771 N=761 N=768 N=228\nQTc 450-480 ms | 82 (10.6) 86 (11.3) 94 (12.2) 24 (10.5)\nQTc 481-500 ms | 29 (3.8) 29 (3.8) 18 (2.3) 4 (1.8)\nQTc >501 ms | 21 (2.7) 13 (1.7) 12 (1.6) 10 (4.4)\nPost-infusion ECG N=765 N=755 N=758 N=224\nQTc 450-480 ms | 96 (12.5) 96 (12.7) 108 (14.2) | 33 (14.3)\nQTc 481-500 ms | 26 (3.4) 25 (3.3) 13 (1.7) 3 (1.3)\nQTc >501 ms | 17 (2.2) 16 (2.1) 15 (2) 13 (5.8)\nQTc change from BL<30 ms__| 713 (93.2) 692 (91.7) 711 (93.8) | 203 (90.6)\nQTc change from BL >30- 35 (4.6) 43 (5.7) 32 (4.2) 15 (6.7)\n<60msec\nQTc change from BL>60ms 10 (1.3) 15 (2) 15(2) 4 (1.8)\n\nms=milliseconds; BL=baseline; QTc= Frederica-corrected QT interval\n\nReviewer Comment: There were numerically more subjects in the bezlo and acto arm with\n\nbaseline and post-baseline OTc 481-500ms; otherwise, there were no significant differences in\nthe number of subjects with OT changes from baseline when comparing the active arms (bezlo\nand acto+bezilo) vs. the placebo arm.\n\n8.4.10. Immunogenicity\n\nDevelopment of anti-drug antibody (ADA), anti-bezlotoxumab antibody, were assessed at\nbaseline (Day1), visits 4 (Day29), 5 (Day57), and 6 (Day 85). In addition, in the extension subset\nof 300 subjects in study P002, presence of ADA was assessed on Day168. Summary of the\nresults of these tests are summarized in Table 63.\n\nTable 63: Presence of Anti-drug Antibody (ADA) in study P001 and P002\n\nTrial Study | Subjects\narm dosed\nbezlo\n\nTotal\nevaluable*\nN\n\nNegative*! | Inconclusive*?\n\nN (%) N (%)\n\nNon-TE\nPositive*?\nN (%)\n\nTE-\nPositive**\nN (%)\n\nReference ID: 3989587\n\n110\n\n\f\nClinical Review\nShrimant Mishra, MD, MPH (Efficacy)\nHiwot, Hiruy, MD, PhD (Safety)\nBLA 761046\nBezlotoxumab\n\n387\n356\n237(66.6)\n114(32)\n5(1.4)\n0(0)\n\nP001\nBezlo\n390\n351\n239(68.1)\n112(31.9)\n0(0)\n0(0)\nActo+\nBezlo\n\n348\n348\n266(76.4)\n78(22.4)\n4(1.1)\n0(0)\n\nP002\nBezlo\n359\n359\n271(75.5)\n88(24.5)\n0(0)\n0(0)\nActo+\nBezlo\n\n1563\n1414\n1013(71.6)\n392(27.7)\n9(0.6)\n0(0)\n\nP001+\nP002\n\n*:Includes patients with at least one ADA sample available after treatment with bezlo or \nacto+bezlo\n#: Denominator was total number of evaluable subjects; TE=Treatment Emergent\n1: Negative result is defined as all pre- and post-treatment samples were negative in the ADA \nconfirmatory assays and the drug concentration in the last post-dose sample was below the \nrespective drug tolerance level (DTL) for the ADA assay\n2: Inconclusive is defined as all pre-and post-treatment samples were negative in the ADA \nconfirmatory assay and the drug concentration in the last post-dose sample was equal or above \nthe respective DTL for the ADA assay\n3: TE- positive result is defined as pre-treatment sample was negative and at least one post-\ntreatment sample was positive in the ADA confirmatory assay (Treatment-induced positive) OR \npre-and post-treatment treatment samples were both positive in the ADA confirmatory assay \nAND the post-treatment titer increased by ≥2-fold (treatment boosted positive)\n4: Non-TE positive result is defined as pre-treatment sample was positive and post-dose samples \nwere negative in the ADA confirmatory assay OR pre-and post-treatment samples were positive \nin the ADA confirmatory assay with a <2-fold increase in titer in the post-treatment sample\n\nData source: Modified from sponsor’s tables 2.7.2:8 and 2.7.2:10 in Summary of Clinical \nPharmacology Studies section\n\n Reviewer Comment: Among the 710 bezlo subjects that had at least one post-infusion ADA \ntesting, none were reported to have developed ADA, although 28% had inconclusive test results.  \nAmong the 704 subjects that received acto+bezlo, there were no subjects reported to have post \ntreatment ADA; however, 9(1.3%) had non-treatment emergent ADA.\n \nIn this review, several factors were considered regarding potential immunogenicity of bezlo, \nincluding patient-specific factors, route of administration, and origin of the study product. The \nintravenous administration of bezlo, its fully human origin, and its target being a non-\nendogenous makes it less likely candidate for eliciting immunogenicity.  In addition, pre-clinical \nstudies of repeated dose administration and tissue cross-reactivity studies did not show \nsignificant immunogenicity. Please refer to Dr. Terry Miller’s, pharmacology-toxicology review, \nfor details of pre-clinical studies. The results of the ADA testing are consistent with the \nexpectation of low immunogenicity; however, some drug reactions are not related to \npharmacological activity of the drug and are not predictable. Thus, continued \n\n111\n\nReference ID: 3989587\n\n\f\nClinical Review\n\nShrimant Mishra, MD, MPH (Efficacy)\nHiwot, Hiruy, MD, PhD (Safety)\n\nBLA 761046\n\nBezlotoxumab\n\npharmacovigilance is needed in the post marketing setting. In addition, the present data only\nsupports low immunogenicity of bezlotoxumab when given as one-time dose. Only 30 subjects in\none Phase I study received two doses of bezlotoxumab; hence, the potential immunogenicity of\nbezlotoxumab after multiple doses cannot be assessed with the available data.\n\n8.5. Analysis of Submission-Specific Safety Issues\n\nInfusion-related adverse events were of particular interest since bezlo is a monoclonal antibody.\nThe sponsor defined post-infusion reaction as any of the following symptoms occurring within\n24 hours post-infusion: infusion-site adverse experiences, pyrexia, chills, rash, arthralgia,\nmyalgia, joint swelling, obstructive airways disorder, bronchospasm, stridor, dysphonia,\nheadache, fatigue, pruritus, urticaria, hypotension, hypertension, nasal congestion, nausea,\nvomiting, flushing, angioedema, dyspnea, and dizziness/lightheadedness. The sponsor used the\nDivision of Microbiology and Infectious Diseases (DMID) Adult Toxicity Table (November\n2007, draft) grading for the reporting of these adverse events.\n\nThis reviewer looked at clinical signs and symptoms of infusion —related reaction that occurred\nwithin the 24 hours following infusion of study drugs. These include:\n\ne Cutaneous -infusion-site reaction, rash, pruritus, urticaria, flushing, angioedema\n\ne Respiratory -dyspnea, bronchospasm, stridor, dysphonia, sudden nasal congestion,\n\nwheeze, sensation of throat closure or choking\n\ne Cardiovascular-faintness, tachycardia, bradycardia, chest pain, hypotension,\nhypertension, sense of impending doom\nGastrointestinal-nausea, vomiting, diarrhea\nMusculoskeletal -arthralgia, myalgia, joint swelling\nNervous system-headache, loss of consciousness, dizziness/lightheadedness\nGeneral conditions-Body temperature alteration(pyrexia, diaphoresis, chills), fatigue,\nand asthenia\nSampson’s criteria of a combination of signs/symptoms from the different organ systems were\napplied to focus on subjects who potentially experienced infusion-related reactions.\n\n8.5.1. Infusion-related Reactions\n\nFor this analysis, adverse events that started on day of infusion (day 1) and the following\nday (day 2) were evaluated. Comparing the occurrence of infusion-related reactions as\ndefined above, 261 subjects (n=106 bezlo, n=77 actot+bezlo, n=78 placebo) had at least one\nAEs that appeared to be infusion-related. Summary of incidence of TEAE and infusion-\nrelated AEs are presented in\n\n112\n\nReference ID: 3989587\n\n\f\nClinical Review\nShrimant Mishra, MD, MPH (Efficacy)\nHiwot, Hiruy, MD, PhD (Safety)\nBLA 761046\nBezlotoxumab\n\nTable 64.\n\nTable 64: Summary of TEAEs and Infusion -related AEs during Days 1 and 2 After \nInfusion\n\nBezlo\n\nPlacebo\n\nTotal\n\nN=786, \nn (%)\n\nActo+\nBezlo\nN=777\nn (%)\n\nN=781\nn (%)\n\nN=2344\nN, (%)\n\nSubjects with any TEAE on day 1 or day 2\n148 (18.8)\n120 (15.4)\n114 (14.6)\n382 (16.3)\n\nSubjects with serious TEAEs \n7 (0.9)\n8 (1.03)\n9 (1.2)\n24 (1)\nSubjects with infusion related AE*\n106 (13.5)\n77 (9.9)\n78(10)\n261 (11.1)\nSubjects with serious AE\n2(0.2)\n4 (0.5)\n3 (0.4)\n9 (0.4)\n*Reviewer’s definition of infusion related AEs as defined in section 8.5 were used. \n\nIn the bezlo arm, there was one subject that had respiratory and gastrointestinal symptoms: this \nwas a 72-year-old man with significant medical history for coronary and peripheral artery \ndisease, hypertension, hyperlipidemia who was admitted for abdominal pain and diarrhea, and \nwas found to have C. difficile infection and pancolitis. He has a small brownish emesis prior to \ninfusion of bezlo that was not known to the study staff prior to infusion bezlo. Following the \ninfusion, he had two small brown-colored emesis. His vital signs and respiratory status were \nstable during infusion. However, he had an acute respiratory decompensation that necessitated \nintubation less than 24 hours after infusion of bezlo. He was taken to the OR emergently for \ncolectomy. The subject did well initially but had complications that required additional surgical \nintervention, and subsequently died 25 days after infusion of bezlo.\n\nNo other bezlo subject fulfilled the combination of cutaneous, respiratory, cardiac, and \ngastrointestinal symptoms outlined in Sampson’s criteria for signs and symptoms of anaphylaxis. \nFour subjects in the placebo arm and one subject in the acto+bezlo arm had cutaneous and \ngastrointestinal symptoms. The acto+bezlo subject was an 83-year-old Japanese male who had \nreported nausea and pruritus about an hour prior to infusion.  \n\nReviewer Comment: The symptoms of the bezlo subject described in the above synopsis appear \nto have been related to his C. difficile colitis and less likely due to infusion reaction. There were \n4 subjects with GI and skin manifestations, mostly pruritus.  The cutaneous symptoms may be \ndue to other concomitant medication the subjects were receiving and the GI symptoms may \npartly be due to CDI’s clinical manifestation.  \n\n113\n\nReference ID: 3989587\n\n\f\nClinical Review\n\nShrimant Mishra, MD, MPH (Efficacy)\nHiwot, Hiruy, MD, PhD (Safety)\n\nBLA 761046\n\nBezlotoxumab\n\nPlease refer to section 8.4.7 for details of subjects that fulfilled Sampson’s criteria of decrease\nblood pressure.\n\nIn the adverse event dataset, there were seven (n=2 bezlo, n=1 acto+bezlo, n=4 placebo) subjects\nwho were diagnosed with hypotension on the day of or the day after infusion (days 1 and 2). A\nsynopsis for the two subjects in the bezlo arm is presented below:\n\nThe first subject was a 76-year-old male with diabetes with baseline BP of 118/51 mmHg, HR\n89 bpm, RR 14, temperature of 37.3C; at 30-min post start of infusion, his vital signs were BP\n133/65 mmHg, HR 100, RR 13, temp 37.6, and end of infusion BP of 103/57 mmHg, HR 100\nbpm, RR 12, temp 37.6C. The reported low blood pressure lasted for one day.\n\nThe second subject was a 76-year-old Native American female with diabetes, atrial fibrillation\nhypothyroidism, and primary squamous cell. Her pre-infusion vital signs were BP 122/54\nmmHg, HR 75 bpm, temp of 36.8C; 30 minutes after start of infusion, her blood pressure\ndropped to 92/49 mmHg; HR was 77 bpm. The low blood pressure lasted for 10 minutes and she\nrecovered with the following vitals at end of infusion: BP of 135/48 mmHg, HR of 84 bpm, and\ntemperature of 36.9 C.\n\nNeither of these two subjects’ decrease in blood pressure fulfilled the Sampson’s criteria of\nanaphylaxis.\n\nReviewer Comment: In addition to the two subjects reported above, there was one additional\nsubjects described in section 8.4.7 that had decrease in blood pressure. However, parsing out\nthe contribution of ongoing infection to the decrease in blood pressure is difficult. Also, the one\nsubject described in section 8.4.3 who developed ventricular arrhythmia, chills and dizziness is\nconsidered to have infusion reaction. In total, there were 4 potential infusion-related reactions.\n\n8.5.2. Safety Analysis by Body Weight\n\nSince bezlo is dosed by body weight, potential effects of higher body weight, and overall higher\ndose, was assessed. Table 65 summarizes the results of this analysis.\n\nTable 65: Safety Analysis by Body Weight\n\nBody weight <70kg Body weight >70kg Total\nN=1233 N=1111 N=2344\nn, (%) n, (%) n, (%)\nAll TEAE 870 (70.6) 752 (67.7) 1622 (69.2)\nBezlo | 283 (23) 265 (23.9) 548 (23.4)\nActot+Bezlo | 295 (23.7) 234 (21.1) 529 (22.6)\n\nReference ID: 3989587\n\n114\n\n\f\nClinical Review\n\nShrimant Mishra, MD, MPH (Efficacy)\nHiwot, Hiruy, MD, PhD (Safety)\n\nBLA 761046\nBezlotoxumab\n\nPlacebo | 292 (23.7) 253 (22.8) 545 (23.3)\nSerious AE 387 (31.4) 311 (28) 698 (29.8)\nBezlo | 122 (9.9) 109 (9.8) 231 (9.9)\nActo+Bezlo | 128 (10.4) 84 (7.6) 212 (9)\nPlacebo | 137 (11.1) 118 (10.6) 255 (10.9)\nDeath 121 (9.8) 45 (4.1) 166 (7.1)\nBezlo | 34 (2.8) 22 (2) 56 (2.4)\nActo+Bezlo | 38 (3.1) 13 (1.2) 51 (2.2)\nPlacebo | 49 (4) 10 (0.9) 59 (2.5)\n\nTEAE: All subjects with at least one TEAE during the 12-week study period\nSAE: All subjects with at least one serious AE during the 12-week study period\n\nDeath: All subjects who died due to an AE that occurred during the 12-week study period\nBezlo: Bezlotoxumab; Acto+Bezlo: Actoxumab and Bezlotoxumab\n\nReviewer’s Comments: There were numerically higher number of TEAE, SAE, and death in\nsubjects weighing <70kgs as compared to subjects weighing >70kg. The extreme low weight\nsubjects potentially being malnourished may partly explain this. As reported in Table 35, there\nwere subjects with body weight as low as 29.8kg.\n\n8.5.3. Safety analysis by baseline Congestive heart failure\nThere were numerically more subjects in the bezlo arm who were reported to have congestive\n\nheart failure as SAE, and subgroup analysis of subjects with baseline CHF for each study arm is\npresented below in Table 66 and Table 67.\n\nTable 66: Subgroup Analysis by Baseline Congestive Heart Failure (CHF)\n\nBaseline CHF No baseline CHF Total\nN=325 N=2019 N=2344\nn,(%) n, (%) n, (%)\nTEAE 250 (66.9) 1372 (68) 1622 (69.2)\nBezlo | 99 (30.5) 449 (22.2) 548 (23.4)\nActo+Bezlo | 78 (24) 451 (22.3) 529 (22.6)\nPlacebo | 73 (22.5) 472 (23.4) 545 (23.3)\nSAE 159 (48.9) 539 (26.7) 698(30)\n\nReference ID: 3989587\n\n115\n\n\f\nClinical Review\n\nShrimant Mishra, MD, MPH (Efficacy)\nHiwot, Hiruy, MD, PhD (Safety)\n\nBLA 761046\n\nBezlotoxumab\n\nBezlo | 63 (19.4) 168 (8.3) 231 (9.9)\nActo+Bezlo | 46 (14.2) 166 (8.2) 212 (9)\nPlacebo | 50 (15.4) 205 (10.2) 255 (10.9)\nDeath 54 (16.6) 112 (5.5) 166 (7.1)\nBezlo | 23 (7.1) 33 (1.6) 56 (2.4)\nActo+Bezlo | 18 (5.5) 33 (1.6) 51 (2.2)\nPlacebo | 13 (4) 46 (2.3) 59 (2.5)\n\nBaseline CHF by arm: Bezlo, n=118; actotbezlo, n=103; placebo, n=104\nTEAE: Treatment emergent adverse event; SAE: serious adverse event\n\nTable 67: Reported Adverse Events among Subjects with Baseline Congestive Heart\nFailure (CHF) status\n\nBezlo Actotbezlo Placebo\n\nN=118 N=103 N=104\n\nn, % n,% n,(%)\nTEAE 99 (83.9) 78 (75.7) 73 (70.2)\nSAE 63 (53.4) 46 (44.7) 50 (48)\nDeath 23 (19.5) 18 (17.5) 13(12.5)\n\nTEAE: All subjects with at least one TEAE during the 12-week study period;\n\nSAE: All subjects with at least one serious AE during the 12-week study period\n\nDeath: All subjects who died due to an AE that occurred during the 12-week study period\nBezlo: Bezlotoxumab:; Acto+Bezlo: Actoxumab and Bezlotoxumab\n\nReviewer’s Comment: It appears that patients with baseline CHF had more AEs, SAEs and\ndeath, in general. Furthermore, there was a distinct numerical difference in the occurrence of\nAEs, SAEs, and death among the bezlo-treated subjects with baseline CHF.\n\n8.6. Safety Analyses by Demographic Subgroups\n\nSubgroup analysis of adverse events by sex, race, and age are presented in Table 68, Table 69,\nand Table 70, respectively. Of note, in Table 69, due to the small numbers of subjects in each\nnon-white racial group, the data for the non-white racial groups were pulled and presented\ntogether.\n\nTable 68: Safety Analysis by Sex\n\n| Female | Male | Total\n\nReference ID: 3989587\n\n116\n\n\f\nClinical Review\nShrimant Mishra, MD, MPH (Efficacy)\nHiwot, Hiruy, MD, PhD (Safety)\nBLA 761046\nBezlotoxumab\n\nN=1327\nN=1017\nN=2344\n\nTEAE\n943 (71)\n679 (66.8)\n1622 (69.2)\n\nBezlo\n318 (24)\n230 (22.6)\n548 (23.4)\n\nActo+Bezlo\n302 (22.8)\n227 (22.3)\n529 (22.6)\n\nPlacebo\n323 (24.3)\n222 (21.8)\n545 (23.3)\n\nSAE\n369 (27.8)\n329 (32.4)\n698 (29.8)\n\nBezlo\n122 (9.2)\n109 (10.7)\n231 (9.9)\n\nActo+Bezlo\n108 (8.1)\n104 (10.2)\n212 (9)\n\nPlacebo\n139 (10.5)\n116 (11.4)\n255 (10.9)\n\nDeath\n83 (6.3)\n83 (8.2)\n166 (7.1)\nBezlo\n25 (1.9)\n31 (3)\n56 (2.4)\nActo+Bezlo\n23 (1.7)\n28 (2.8)\n51 (2.2)\n\nPlacebo\n35 (2.6)\n24 (2.4)\n59 (2.5)\n\nTEAE: All subjects with at least one TEAE during the 12-week study period\nSAE:  All subjects with at least one serious AE during the 12-week study period\nDeath: All subjects who died due to an AE that occurred during the 12-week study period\nBezlo: Bezlotoxumab; Acto+Bezlo: Actoxumab and Bezlotoxumab. \n\nTable 69: Safety Analysis by Race\n\nNon-white*\nN=352\n\nWhite\nN=1992\n\nTotal\nN=2344\n\nn, (%)\n\nn, (%)\n\nn, (%)\n\nTEAE\n250 (71)\n1372 (84.6)\n1622 (69.2)\n\nBezlo\n96 (27.3)\n452 (27.9)\n548 (23.4)\n\nActo+Bezlo\n81 (23)\n448 (27.6)\n529 (22.6)\n\nPlacebo\n73 (20.7)\n472 (29.1)\n545 (23.3)\n\nSerious AE\n127 (36.1)\n571 (35.2)\n698 (29.8)\n\n117\n\nReference ID: 3989587\n\n\f\nClinical Review\nShrimant Mishra, MD, MPH (Efficacy)\nHiwot, Hiruy, MD, PhD (Safety)\nBLA 761046\nBezlotoxumab\n\nBezlo\n42 (11.9)\n189 (11.7)\n231 (9.9)\n\nActo+Bezlo\n46 (13.1)\n166 (10.2)\n212 (9)\n\nPlacebo\n39 (11.1)\n216 (13.3)\n255 (10.9)\n\nDeath\n26 (7.4)\n140 (8.6)\n166 (7.1)\n\nBezlo\n8 (2.3)\n48 (3)\n56 (2.4)\n\nActo+Bezlo\n10 (2.8)\n41 (2.5)\n51 (2.2)\n\nPlacebo\n8 (2.3)\n51 (3.1)\n59 (2.5)\n\n*Non-whit races include African American or black, American Indian or Alaskan Native, Asian \nand multi-racial subjects\nTEAE: All subjects with at least one TEAE during the 12-week study period\nSAE:  All subjects with at least one serious AE during the 12-week study period\nDeath: All subjects who died due to an AE that occurred during the 12-week study period\nBezlo: Bezlotoxumab; Acto+Bezlo: Actoxumab and Bezlotoxumab\n\nReviewer Comment: There is no significant difference in the proportion of TEAEs, SAEs and \ndeaths when comparing non-white subjects to white subject across each arm, or comparing men \nand women. \n\nTable 70: Safety Analysis by Age\n\nAge<65 years\nN=1100\nn, (%)\n\nAge≥65 years\nN=1244\nn, (%)\n\nTotal\nN=2344\nn, (%)\n\nAll TEAE\n739 (67.2)\n883 (71)\n1622(69.2)\nBezlo\n270 (24.5)\n278 (22.3)\n548 (23.4)\nActo+Bezlo\n221 (20.1)\n308 (24.8)\n529 (22.6)\nPlacebo\n248 (22.5)\n297 (23.9)\n545 (23.3)\nSAE\n260 (23.6)\n438 (35.2)\n698 (29.8)\nBezlo\n98 (8.9)\n133 (10.7)\n231 (9.9)\nActo+Bezlo\n69 (6.3)\n143 (11.5)\n212 (9)\nPlacebo\n93 (8.5)\n162 (13)\n255 (10.9)\nDeath\n40 (3.6)\n126 (10.1)\n166 (7.1)\nBezlo\n15 (1.4)\n41 (3.3)\n56 (2.4)\nActo+Bezlo\n10 (0.9)\n41 (3.3)\n51 (2.2)\nPlacebo\n15 (1.4)\n44 (3.3)\n59 (2.5)\n\n118\n\nReference ID: 3989587\n\n\f\nClinical Review\n\nShrimant Mishra, MD, MPH (Efficacy)\nHiwot, Hiruy, MD, PhD (Safety)\n\nBLA 761046\n\nBezlotoxumab\n\nTEAE: All subjects with at least one TEAE during the 12-week study period\n\nSAE: All subjects with at least one serious AE during the 12-week study period\n\nDeath: All subjects who died due to an AE that occurred during the 12-week study period\nBezlo: Bezlotoxumab; Acto+Bezlo: Actoxumab and Bezlotoxumab\n\nReviewer Comment: There were more SAEs and deaths in subjects >65 years old as compared to\n<65 years in all treatment arms including placebo. Older age is one of the prognostic factors for\nsevere CDI. Hence, it is likely that the results are reflecting that. However, there were no\nsignificant differences in adverse outcomes between the bezlo arm as compared to the placebo\narm in each age cohort.\n\n8.7. Specific Safety Studies/Clinical Trials\n\nThere was no specific study or clinical trial that was conducted to evaluate a specific safety\nconcern.\n\n8.8. Additional Safety Explorations\n8.8.1. Human Carcinogenicity or Tumor Development\nThis subsection is not applicable.\n8.8.2. Human Reproduction and Pregnancy\n\nThere was one pregnancy reported in study P002, and the narrative is included below. It occurred\nin a 23 year old, multi-racial (African American and white) subject with a negative result on a\nserum pregnancy test prior to receiving infusion with acto+bezlo. On day 10, she presented to an\nemergency room with dizziness, abdominal cramping, nausea, and occasional retching. She was\ndischarged from the hospital with a diagnosis of moderate, persistent CDI. She subsequently had\npositive urine and a confirmatory serum pregnancy test. On day 258, the subject had a\nspontaneous, live, vaginal birth of a healthy, female baby with normal anthropometric\nmeasurements.\n\n8.8.3. Pediatrics and Assessment of Effects on Growth\n\nThe pediatric population was excluded from the Phase 3 trials. The sponsor submitted an initial\nPediatric Study Plan (iPSP) on January 28, 2014 for acto+bezlo. The submission included a\ndeferral of pediatric assessments until after approval of the Biologics Licensing Application\n(BLA) in adults. Subsequently, after consulting with the FDA, the sponsor submitted an Agreed\nAmended iPSP for bezlo (referred to as MK-6072) on November 6, 2015 as bezlo became the\nselected product for marketing authorization (eCTD sequence number 0291).\n\n119\n\nReference ID: 3989587\n\n\f\nClinical Review\n\nShrimant Mishra, MD, MPH (Efficacy)\nHiwot, Hiruy, MD, PhD (Safety)\n\nBLA 761046\n\nBezlotoxumab\n\n8.8.4. Overdose, Drug Abuse Potential, Withdrawal, and Rebound\n\nOverall, there were 1790 subjects exposed to bezlo either alone or in combination with acto; 126\nwere healthy subjects in Phase 1 (30 subjects received bezlo alone and 96 received acto + bezlo),\nand 1664 were subjects in Phase 2 and 3 (786 received bezlotoxumab alone and 878 received\nactot+bezlo). Of these, 1741 subjects were exposed to bezlo dose at 10mg/kg, while 19 were\nexposed to 20mg/kg. With the exception of subjects in study P004 who received two doses of\nactot+bezlo (n=30), all subjects were exposed to a single dose of bezlo or actotbezlo.\n\nSince bezlo is a human monoclonal antibody, subsection on potential for abuse, withdrawal and\nrebound are not applicable.\n\n8.9. Safety in the Postmarket Setting\n\n8.9.1. Safety Concerns Identified Through Postmarket Experience\n\nThe study drug is a novel biologic, and thus, there is no postmarket experience.\n\n8.9.2. Expectations on Safety in the Postmarket Setting\n\nSince the mechanism of action for this monoclonal antibody is not fully elucidated, it is difficult\nto anticipate all the potential off-target effects. Hence, postmarketing pharmacovigilance is\nrecommended.\n\nIn addition, there are only 29 subjects that received more than one dose of bezlo in a Phase 1\nstudy of healthy volunteers. Hence, there is minimal data as to the safety of repeated doses of\nbezlo. In the post-marketing setting, it is possible that patients may receive more than one dose\nof bezlo, especially patients who attend multiple health care facilities.\n\nAs mentioned earlier, there is paucity of minorities in the Phase 3 trials. Although the subgroup\n\nanalysis based on ethnicity did not show major differences in AE rates or mortality, the small\nnumbers may not allow for detection of some safety signals in subpopulation of minority groups.\n\n8.10. Additional Safety Issues From Other Disciplines\n\nThere were no safety concems raised by product quality, microbiology, and clinical\npharmacology and pharmacology-toxicology disciplines.\n120\n\nReference ID: 3989587\n\n\f\nClinical Review\n\nShrimant Mishra, MD, MPH (Efficacy)\nHiwot, Hiruy, MD, PhD (Safety)\n\nBLA 761046\n\nBezlotoxumab\n\n8.11. Integrated Assessment of Safety\n\nSummary\n\nBezlotoxumab, given as a single IV infusion, had an overall favorable safety profile with the\nrates of treatment emergent adverse events (TEAE), serious adverse events (SAE) and deaths\nsimilar to placebo. No increase in adverse events (AEs) related to potential immune-mediated\nreactions was observed in the bezlotoxumab- treated subjects as compared to placebo- treated\nsubjects. A safety finding identified during the review of data from the two Phase 3 trials is the\nnumerically higher numbers of serious adverse events and deaths in bezlotoxumab-treated\nsubjects with baseline congestive heart failure as compared to placebo.\n\nDiscussion\n\nThere was adequate number of subjects exposed to bezlo at the proposed dose during the drug\ndevelopment program. There were 1790 subjects exposed to bezlo either alone or in\ncombination with acto; 126 were healthy subjects in Phase 1 studies (30 subjects received bezlo\nalone and 96 received acto + bezlo), and 1664 were subjects in Phase 2 and 3 (786 received\nbezlotoxumab alone and 878 received actot+bezlo). Of these, 1741 subjects were exposed to\nbezlo dose at 10mg/kg. With the exception of subjects in study P004 who received two doses of\nactot+bezlo (n=30), all subjects were exposed to a single dose of bezlo or actotbezlo.\n\nThe safety population is comprised of all subjects in the two Phase 3 studies, studies P0O1 and\nP002), that received partial or full infusion of the study medication. For the safety analysis,\nsubjects were grouped in the treatment arm they received (as treated), as opposed to the group\nthey were randomized to. Throughout the two pivotal studies, POO1 and P002, there were 212\ndeaths. Among these, 166 occurred in the safety population because of AEs reported during the\n12-week study period. There was no significant difference in the mortality rate of the bezlo\n(7.1%) and acto+bezlo (6.6%) arm as compared to placebo (7.1%).\n\nThere is no major imbalance in the frequency of serious adverse events among the three arms:\n29.4%, 27.3% o and 32.7% of subjects in the bezlo, acto+bezlo and placebo arm, respectively,\nexperienced at least one SAE in the 12-week study period. In all three arms, the infection,\ninfestation, and gastrointestinal MedDRA System Organ Classes accounted for the majority of\nthe SAEs. A detailed look of the SAEs revealed cardiac failure, urinary tract infection, diarrhea,\nabdominal pain to have occurred more frequently in the bezlo arm as compared to the placebo\narm. From these, cardiac failure showed the largest risk difference, with 17 subjects (2.2%) in\nthe bezlo arm as compared to placebo (n=7, 0.009%). Among subjects with baseline diagnosis of\ncongestive heart failure, more subjects in the bezlo died (23/119 vs. 13/104).\n\n121\n\nReference ID: 3989587\n\n\f\nClinical Review\nShrimant Mishra, MD, MPH (Efficacy)\nHiwot, Hiruy, MD, PhD (Safety)\nBLA 761046\nBezlotoxumab\n\nThere were 47 (n=9 bezlo, n=34 acto+bezlo, n=4 placebo) infusion interruptions. The majority of \nthese were due to technical difficulties. Only one subject in the bezlo arm discontinued study \ninfusion due to adverse reactions of ventricular tachycardia, chills, and dizziness. \n\nAnalysis to evaluate occurrence infusion-related reactions did not reveal a significant difference \nin adverse events that occurred on days 1 and 2 after infusion among the study arms. Looking at \nchanges in systolic pressure over the infusion period, numerically more subjects had either \ndecrease in SBP by 30% from baseline or SBP<90mmHg when comparing  the bezlo arm  at 30 \nminutes after start of infusion (10 vs. 8) and end of infusion (13 vs. 7).  In total, there were 4 \nsubjects with potential infusion-related reactions. \n\nAmong the 710 bezlo subjects that had at least one post-infusion anti-drug antibody (ADA) \ntesting, none were reported to have developed ADA, although 28% had inconclusive test results. \nThe subgroup analysis of adverse events by age, race, sex and body weight did not reveal \nsignificant differences by the abovementioned variables.  \n\nOverall, one time dose of bezlo appears safe; however, safety, including, immunogenicity, of \nmultiple doses of bezlo cannot be assessed based on the data provided. \n\n9\nAdvisory Committee Meeting and Other External Consultation\n\nAn Advisory Committee (AC) meeting was held on June 9th, 2016.  The AC consisted of \ninfectious disease specialists, pharmacologists, statisticians, a gastroenterologist, and patient \nrepresentatives, as well as key division and office personnel. The following key findings were \nnoted:\n\n-\nOn the single voting question of “Has the applicant provided substantial evidence of \nthe safety and effectiveness of bezlotoxumab for the prevention of C. difficile \ninfection recurrence in patients aged 18 years and older?,” there were 10 votes for \nYes, 5 votes for No, and 1 abstention.\n-\nMany members felt that benefit with regards to recurrence had been demonstrated \nthough some members had concerns as to any potential negative impact on clinical \ncure.\n-\nSome committee members were concerned about particular trial design \ncharacteristics, such as a primary endpoint based on recurrence and the receipt of \nstudy drug while also receiving initial SOC treatment.\n-\nSeveral committee members recommended a warning related to use in subjects with \nunderlying CHF.\n-\nSome committee members felt that benefit had not been demonstrated and that an \nadditional trial to resolve the regulatory and clinical issues could easily be conducted\n\n122\n\nReference ID: 3989587\n\n\f\nClinical Review\nShrimant Mishra, MD, MPH (Efficacy)\nHiwot, Hiruy, MD, PhD (Safety)\nBLA 761046\nBezlotoxumab\n\n-\nCommittee members recommended evaluating further the impact of the drug on the \nseverity of recurrences as well as evaluating the clinical significance of subjects noted \nto be initial clinical failures. \n\n10 Labeling Recommendations\n\nA warning on the possibility of cardiac failure in subjects with a history of congestive heart \nfailure is being considered to be included in the bezlotoxumab label. Overall, the discussions of \nthe content of the bezlotoxumab label continue at the time this review is written.   \n\n11  Risk Evaluation and Mitigation Strategies (REMS)\n\n This reviewer does not recommend REMS for this product\n\n12 Postmarketing Requirements and Commitments\n\n123\n\nReference ID: 3989587\n\n\f\nClinical Review\n\nShrimant Mishra, MD, MPH (Efficacy)\nHiwot, Hiruy, MD, PhD (Safety)\n\nBLA 761046\n\nBezlotoxumab\n\nThis reviewer believes a properly designed and conducted PMR study could provide clarity\nregarding the timing of study drug relative to SOC and clarify whether study drug (and its\ntiming) have any effects on clinical cure and recurrences. Given the prevalence of CDI, such a\ntrial should not be difficult to perform.\n\n12.1. References\n\nMandell, Douglas, and Bennett’s principles and practice of infectious diseases/Gerald L.\nMandell, John E. Bennett, Raphael Dolin. 7% ed. 2010\n\n12.2. Financial Disclosure\n\nNone.\n\n124\n\nReference ID: 3989587\n\n\f\n-------------------------------------------------------------